800:
** ἀσύμφων-ος**,
_Attic dialect_ ἀξ-, ον, **not harmonious,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 402d; χορδή 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 11.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **discordant, at variance,** ἐμαυτῷ{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 482c; ἕξεις 1st c.BC(?): Ocellus Lucanus Philosophus 4.13; πρὸς ἀλλήλους NT.Act.28.25, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “An.Prooem.” (_comparative_)}}. _adverb_ **-νως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 860c}}; τοῖς αὐτοῖς argument Str.I.
**II** **not speaking the same language,** πρὸς ἄλληλα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 262d, compare “Lg.” 777d; ἀ. ταῖς διαλέκτοις 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.53.}}

801:
** ἀσύνετος**,
_Attic dialect_ ἀξ-, ον, **void of understanding, witless,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 31}}: _comparative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.81, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 493, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142; φρὴν ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 456}}; τί τάδ᾽ ἀσύνετ; what **folly** is this? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 352 (Lyric poetry)}}. _adverb_ **-τως**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.141b.}}
**2** with _genitive_, **not able to understand,** λόγου{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 1, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.713b, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.218b.}}
**II** **not to be understood, unintelligible,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1205, “Ph.” 1731}}. _adverb_ **-τως**{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.8.11.}}

802:
** ἀσύν-θετος**, ον,
(συντίθημι) **uncompounded,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 78c, “Tht.” 205c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1252a19; frequently in Grammars, as 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 172.27, +others}}; ἀ. φωνή a word **standing alone,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.50}}. _adverb_ **-τως**{{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 17.6.}}
**II** (συντίθεμαι) **bound by no covenant, faithless,** ὁ δῆμός ἐστιν πρᾶγμα τῶν πάντων ἀσυνθετώτατον[{{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.136 (variant{ἀσυνετ-}),cf. NT.Rom.1.31}}]; **making no covenants,** ἀ. διατελοῦσι{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1251.19.}}

803:
** ἀσφάλ-εια** [φᾰ],
_genitive_ ας, _Ionic dialect_ ης, ἡ, (ἀσφᾰλής) **security against stumbling** or **falling,** ἀ. πρὸς τὸν πηλόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.22}}; **steadfastness, stability,** ἀσφαλείᾳ.. ἀνόρθωσον πόλιν raise up the city **so that it stand fast,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 51; κατασκευάζειν τὴν [τῆς πολιτείας] ἀ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1319b39.}}
**2** **assurance from danger, personal safety,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 495, etc.; τηρεῖν ἀ. ἐπιβουλῆς 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.8}}; ἀ. τῆς ἐπαναφορᾶς **precaution regarding** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.33, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.68, 8.4}}; ἡ ἰδία ἀ., opposed to ὁ τῆς πόλεως κίνδυνος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.7; δεηθεὶς τῆς ἀ. ἔτυχε}}**safe-conduct,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.7}}; ἀ. διδόναι, παρέχειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.2.2, “Cyr.” 4.5.28: frequently with Preps., ἀσφαλείης εἵνεκεν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.33; ἀσφαλείας οὕνεκα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 293; δι᾽ ἀσφαλείας τὰς πόλεις οἰκεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.17}}; τὸ σῶμ᾽ ἐν ἀσφαλείᾳ καθιστάναι, καθεστάναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.30, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 2.10}}; κατ᾽ ἀσφάλειαν in **safety,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.128; μετ᾽ ἀσφαλείας [prev. author] 1.120, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 50b: _plural_, ἀσφάλειαι}}**seasons of safety,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.21.}}
**3** **caution,** σῴζονται ὑπ᾽ ἀσφαλείας{{Refs 1st c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.10, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus (?)cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 46.11.27 and 14.4}}: in Lit. Crit., **circumspection,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 287.}}
**4** **assurance, certainty,** ἀ. πολλὴ μὴ ἂν ἐλθεῖν αὐτούς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.11}}; ἀ. ἐργάζεσθαι τὴν γῆν **security** for agriculture, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.4.5.}}
**5** ἀ. λόγου **convincing nature, certainty** of an argument, {{NT|[prev. author] “Mem.” 4.6.15, cf. NT.Luke.1.4.}}
**6** as law-term, **security, bond,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.13.7}}; **pledge**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1149.24 (1st c.BC)}}: in _plural_, = Latin _cautiones,_ {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 72.6.}}
**7** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus}} name for **eight,** {{Refs|Theol.Ar. 56.}}

804:
** ἀσφαλ-ής**, ές,
(σφάλλομαι, σφαλῆναι) **not liable to fall, immovable, steadfast,** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only once as}}_adjective_ (compare infr. III), θεῶν ἕδος ἀ. αἰεί{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.42, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 128, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.3, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.34, etc.; ἀσφαλῆ θεῶν νόμιμα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 454}}; **unshaken,** of purpose, ἀ. ὁ νοῦς{{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 351.}}
**2** of friends and the like, **unfailing, trusty,** οὐ γὰρ οἱ.. εὐρύνωτοι φῶτες ἀσφαλέστατοι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 1251; ἀ. στρατηλάτης 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 599, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.69: with}}_infinitive_, φρονεῖν γὰρ οἱ ταχεῖς οὐκ ἀσφαλεῖς the hasty in counsel are not **safe,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 617, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 231a; σῴζειν τὰ κοινὰ πράγματ᾽ ἀσφαλέσταται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1062}}; of things, **sure, certain,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.108,etc.}}
**3** **assured from danger, safe,** ἀ. αἰών{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.86; ἀσφαλεῖ σὺν ἐξόδῳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1288; ἀ. ὄρος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 12.1; ὁδός -εστέρα [prev. author] “HG” 5.4.51}}; ἐν τῷ ἀσφαλεῖ in **safety,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.137, 8.39, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 892e; ἐν ἀσφαλεῖ τοῦ μὴ παθεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.31; τοῦ λαλεῖν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 25}}; ἐν -εστέρῳ, -εστάτῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.21, “An.” 1.8.22; ἐν ἀ. βίου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 785; μένειν ἐν τῷ ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.7.8}}; ἐξ ἀσφαλοῦς from **a place of safety,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Eq.Mag.” 4.16; τοῦ ἀσφαλέος εἵνεκα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.109}}; τὸ ἀ., ={ἀσφάλεια}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.55, etc.}}; μετὰ τοῦ αὑτῆς ἀ. with **no risk to** herself, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.8.7}}; ἀσφαλές [ἐστι], with _infinitive_, **it is safe** to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.75, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 891, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1489: _absolutely_, ἀλλ᾽ οὐκ ἀσφαλές 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 61d, etc.; φεύγειν αὐτοῖς ἀσφαλέστερόν ἐστιν ἢ ἡμῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.19.}}
**4** ἀ. ῥήτωρ a **convincing** speaker, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mem.” 4.6.15.}}
**5** in Lit. Crit., **sound, not risky,** of language or rhythm, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 19, 41.}}_adverb_ -ῶς, ἐρεῖ{{Refs|[prev. work] 78.}}
**II** _substantive_ ἀσφαλές, τό, ={ἀσφάλεια}{{Refs 4th c.AD+|6, “BGU” 984.14 (4th c.AD), etc.}}
**III** _Epic dialect_ _adverb_ ἀσφαλέως, ἔχειν, μένειν, to be, remain **firm, steady,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.325, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.235: _neuter_ ἀσφαλές}} as _adverb_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” (see. below); δρακεῖσ᾽ ἀσφαλές 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.20; ἀ. ἀγορεύει}}**without faltering,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.171, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 86; ἔμπεδον ἀσφαλέως 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.141, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.86; ἔμπεδον ἀσφαλὲς αἰεί 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.683.}}_adverb_ ἀσφαλῶς (-έως) is used in all senses of the _adjective_, -έως βεβηκὼς ποσσί{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 58.4}}; **in safety, with certainty,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 613; ἀ. βουλεύειν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.34; ἀ. ἔχει 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86}}: with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 27.6; ἀ. προσθεῖναι}}**as a precaution,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteorologicorum libros commentaria - pars” 14.10}}: _comparative_ **-έστερον**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.161, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 85d}} but **-εστέρως** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.15, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.71}}: _superlative_ **-έστατα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.23, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 467e.}}

805:
** ἀσφᾰλίζω**
{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.30.3}}:—mostly in _middle_, _future_ **-ιοῦμαι**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.21.4}} but **-ίσομαι** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “AJ” 13.5.11, and so 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.24}}: _perfect_ ἠσφάλισμαι{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.43.6:}}_pluperfect_ ἠσφάλιστο{{Refs|[prev. work] 7.12}}: _aorist_ ἠσφαλισάμην{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] 2.22.11; ἠσφαλίσθην 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Vita” 62}}: some of these tenses are used in _passive_ sense (see. below):—**fortify,** τὸν τόπον{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.30.3, etc.}}:—_passive_, {{NT|[prev. author] 1.42.7, 4.70.9, NT.Matt.27.64.}}
**b** **secure**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“BGU” 829.9 (1st c.AD).}}
**2** more frequently in _middle_, **secure,** ἑαυτόν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 215}}; τὰς εἰσβολάς, τὴν χώραν, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.65.6, 4.60.5, etc.; τόπους 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Belopoeica” 101.7; τὸν ὕσπληγγα “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2824 (from Aphrodisias)}}; **shut up, close,** πύλην{{LXX|LXX.Neh.3.15}}; ὀφθαλμοὶ ἠσφαλισμένοι, opposed to ἀνεῳγμένοι, {{Refs|Polemo Physiognomonicus 55}}; τοὺς πόδας ἠσφαλίσατο εἰς τὸ ξύλον **made** them **fast,** {{NT|NT.Acts.16.24.}}
**3** **secure the person of, arrest,** τινά{{Refs 1st c.BC+|“PTeb.” 283.19 (1st c.BC),compare “PRyl.” 68.19 (1st c.BC, _passive_)}}; **seize,** τὰ γενήματα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work] 53.29 (2nd c.BC).}}
**4** _middle_, **certify,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 2.77.40 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** _middle_, **secure oneself against, ward off,** τὰς καταφορὰς τῶν μαχαιρῶν{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.23.4, compare 9.3.3}}: _absolutely_, **safeguard oneself,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 13.5.10, “POxy.” 1033.13 (4th c.AD).}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_ in Rhet., **safeguard** a risky metaphor, ἀ. τὰς μεταφοράς{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 85:}} — _passive_, λέξις ἠσφαλισμένη τοῖς συνδέσμοις{{Refs|[prev. work] 193. (The word is βάρβαρον _accusative_ to “Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 456.)}}

808:
** ἀσχημ-οσύνη**, ἡ,
**want of form,** ἀ. καὶ ἀμορφία{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 190b15, compare 188b20, 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis de Caelo commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 129.26.}}
**2** **ungracefulness,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 196a, “R.” 401a}}; **awkwardness,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 174c}}; **disfigurement,** τοῦ προσώπου, in playing on the flute, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1341b5.}}
**3** ἀ. φέρει brings **discredit, disgrace,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1126b33.}}
**II** in moral sense, **indecorum, obscene** or **disgraceful conduct,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Rom.1.27: in _plural_, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.78, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 61.31.}}
**III** euphemistic for αἰδοῖον, {{LXX|LXX.Lev.18.7, +others}}; for ἀπόπατος,{{LXX|LXX.Deu.23.13(14).}}

809:
** ἀσχήμ-ων**, ον,
_genitive_ ονος, (σχῆμα) **misshapen, ugly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 27, 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Parmenidem commentarii” p.624S.}}
**II** **unseemly, shameful,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 299, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 46a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1336b14, etc.}}
**II.2** of persons, ἀ. γενέσθαι to be **indecorous,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.160; ἀσχημονέστερος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1127b13.}}
**III** _adverb_ **-νως**{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.12.1, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 29}}: _superlative_ **-έστατα** **very meanly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 959d.}}

810:
** ἀσωτία**, ἡ,
**prodigality, wastefulness,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 560e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1107b10; τὴν ἀ. ὑγρότητα προσαγορεύουσιν 4th c.BC: Crobylus Comicus 4.}}
**2** **profligacy,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Eph.5.18, +others: _plural_, ἐν ἀσωτίαις καὶ κραιπάλαις 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.5.1.}}

811:
Included with: ** ἄσωτος**, ον,
(σῴζω) **having no hope of safety, in desperate case,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 962b5.}}_adverb_ -τως, ἔχειν to be **past recovery,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.918d.}}
**II** in moral sense, **abandoned,** τᾶς ἀσώτου Σισυφιδᾶν γενεᾶς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 189 (Lyric poetry)}}; **spendthrift,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 743b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1107b12, 1120a1, +others}}: _superlative_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 67.6}}; **profligate,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 18.2}}. _adverb_ **-τως**{{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 217a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 40.58, NT.Luke.15.13}}: _comparative_ **-ότερον**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 62.27.}}
**III** _active_, ἄσωτος γένει **bringing destruction on** the race, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1597.}}

812:
** ἀτακτ-έω**,
of a soldier, **to be undisciplined,** opposed to εὐτακτέω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.6, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.11; τοὺς ἀτακτοῦντας τῶν τριηράρχων “IG” 2.809b13}}:—_passive_, πολλὰ γὰρ ἠτάκτητο αὐτοῖς{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.10.10}}: generally, **neglect one's duty, fail to discharge obligation,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Elephantine Papyri 2.13(3rd c.BC), NT.2Thes.3.7, “POxy.” 275.24(1st c.AD).}}
**2** generally, **lead a disorderly life,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.18, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 7.31: with _genitive_, τῆς πατρίου ἀγωγῆς}} **to desert** it, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.235b.}}
**3** **raise a riot** or **rebellion**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“OGI” 200.6 (4th c.AD).}}

813:
** ἄτακτ-ος**, ον,
**not in battle-order,** of troops, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.93, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.105 (_comparative_).}}
**2** **not at one's post,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 39.}}
**II** **undisciplined, disorderly,** θόρυβος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.10; ποιεῖν τὴν πολιτείαν ἀτακτοτέραν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1319b15}}; **irregular,** πυρετός{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 26; οὐδὲν ἄ. τῶν φύσει 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 252a11; φθορὰ ἄ.}}**casual,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 556a12}}; of sensual excess, **irregular, inordinate,** ἡδοναί, Ἀφροδίτη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 660b, 840e}}; in Music, **without rhythm,** μελῳδίαι{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Aristides Quintilianus Musicus 1.13}}; in Medicine texts, **irregular,** σφυγμός{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.458.}}
**II.2** **uncivilized, lawless,** βίος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 25.1 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**II.3** in Mathematics texts, ἄτακτα προβλήματα **indeterminate, not admitting of a definite solution,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in primum Euclidis librum commentarius” p.220 F.}}
**B** _adverb_ -τως **in an irregular, disorderly manner,** of troops, ἀ. καὶ οὐδενὶ κόσμῳ προσπίπτοντες{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.108; ἀ. διώκειν [prev. author] 2.91; ἀτακτότερον προσπεσόντες [prev. author] 6.97, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.22, “Hellenica Oxyrhynchia” 6.4; ἀ. φέρεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.32; οὐθὲν ἀ. θεῷ πράττεται 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.65U.}}
**B.2** **irregularly,** of fevers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.7; ζῆν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.31.}}
**B.3** _comparative_ ἀτακτοτέρως **somewhat negligently,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 53.}}

814:
