815:
** ἄτεκν-ος**, ον,
**without children, childless, barren,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 602, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 828 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 164 (Lyric poetry), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1099b4, “Tab.Heracl.” 1.151, etc.}}; of animals and fishes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 577a3, “GA” 755b19: also with _genitive_, ἄ. ἀρσένων παίδων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1305.}}_adverb_ -νως{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 206.}}
**II** in causal sense, λειχὴν ἄφυλλος ἄ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 785. [ἄτε ¯ κνος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus [same place], ἄτε?~Xκνος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus and 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus [same places]]}}

816:
** ἀτεν-ίζω**,
**look intently, gaze earnestly,** τοῖς ὄμμασιν **stare,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.10; εἴς τι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 343b12; πρός τι [prev. author] “Pr.” 959a24}}; of the eyes, ἀτενίζοντες αὐτῷ{{NT+2nd c.BC+|NT.Luke.4.20, cf. NT.Act.23.1, “Placita Philosophorum” 1.7; εἴς τι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.11.12, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 5.12.3, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.75, etc.; εἴς τινα NT.Act.6.15; εἰς τὸν θεόν 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 4.51b; πρὸς τὸ ἐκείνου πρόσωπον 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 11}}: _absolutely_, also of the eyes, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 957b18}}:—_passive_, **to be gazed upon,** {{Refs|Anthologia Graeca Appx. 4.204 (Praxit.).}}
**II** _metaphorically_ of the mind, ἀ. τὴν διάνοιαν πρός τι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 192a15; εἰς τὴν προαίρεσιν ἀτενίζοντα πράττειν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.96 W.}}; **to be obstinate,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Lynceus Comicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 7.313f.}}

817:
** ἄτερ** [ᾰ],
_Epic dialect_, _Ionic dialect_, Trag. _preposition_ with _genitive_, **without, apart from,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus; κράτιστον Ἀχιλέος ἄ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.27}}; ἄ. Ζηνός **without** his will, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.292, compare “POxy.” 936.18 (3rd c.AD)}}; οὐ θεῶν ἄ. 'non **sine** dis', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.76; ἄ. μόχθου 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 223; ἄ. πυρετοῦ καὶ ὀδύνης 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.4.}}
**II** **aloof, apart from,** ἄ. ἥμενον ἄλλων{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.498; νόσφιν ἄ. τε κακῶν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 91}}:—frequently in Trag., mostly after its case, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 377, etc.}}; but before it in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pr.” 456, “Supp.” 703 (Lyric poetry), “Ch.” 338 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 703 (Lyric poetry), “El.” 866}}:—also in late Prose, as {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.2Mac.12.15, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.10, NT.Luke.22.6, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 5, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 136.9, +others}}
**III** with _dative_, ἄτερ ἄστρασιν{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Anubion Poeta Astrologus 87.}}

818:
** ἀτῑμ-άζω**,
_future_ **-άσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 917 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 465a, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἠτίμασα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 49, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 292e, etc.}}: _perfect_ ἠτίμακα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.31, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 266d}}:—_passive_, _perfect_ ἠτίμασμαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 20, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 219d, 4th c.BC: Ephorus Historicus “fragment”}} 3.21B.: _aorist_ ἠτιμάσθην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 123.5, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 931b}}: _future_ ἀτιμασθήσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1068, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1081}}: (ἄτιμος):—**hold in no honour, esteem lightly,** with _accusative_, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.450 ἀτιμάζεσκε δ᾽ ἄκοιτιν}}; frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”, τούσδε γ᾽ ἀτιμάζει κατὰ δῆμον 6.283; οἶκον ἀτιμάζοντες ἔδουσιν 21.332, compare 427; ἀ. τοκῆας 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 821: frequently in Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1023, “Eu.” 712, 917, +others; μή μ᾽ ἀτιμάσας γένῃ 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista Trag.20 (= [prev. author]Comedy texts 80), compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 40.26, etc.}}; ἀ. καὶ κολάζειν, opposed to ἐπαινεῖν καὶ τιμᾶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.20; τὴν ἀνθρωπίνην ἀσθένειαν ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 107b, +others}}; **bring dishonour upon,** τὴν πόλιν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.31: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἔπη ἃ ἀτιμάζεις πόλιν}} the words thou **speakest in dishonour of** the city, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 340}}:—_passive_, **suffer dishonour, insult,** etc., πρός τινος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 123.5, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.61; τινί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1342; οὐκ ἀτιμασθήσομαι [prev. author] “OT” 1081, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.74}}; τῷ γεγενημένῳ **put to shame by**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.27: with _neuter_ _plural_, ἀνάξι᾽ ἠτιμασμένη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 943.}}
**2** with _genitive_ _of things_, **treat as unworthy of,** μηδ᾽ ἀτιμάσῃς λόγου (i.e. ἐμέ) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 783}}; μή μ᾽ ἀτιμάσῃς ὧν σε προστρέπω φράσαι, ={τούτων ἅ σε πρ. φρ}., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 49, compare “Ant.” 22.}}
**3** with _infinitive_, ὦ θάνατε Παιάν, μή μ᾽ ἀτιμάσῃς μολεῖν **do** not **deem** me **unworthy** of thy visit, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 255.1}}; μήτοι μ᾽ ἀτιμάσῃς τὸ μὴ οὐ θανεῖν σὺν σοί **deem** me not **unworthy** to die, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 544}}; but also οὐκ ἀτιμάσω θεοὺς προσειπεῖν **will** not **disdain** to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 608, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 182c.}}
**II** in legal sense, **disfranchise,** ὑπὸ τῆς πόλεως ἠτιμασμένος{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Ephorus Historicus [same place]}}; at Rome, of the Censors, **punish with ignominia,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 38.13.}}

819:
** ἀτῑμ-ία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, **dishonour, disgrace,** ἀτιμίῃσιν ἰάλλειν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.142, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.21, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1035, etc.; ἐν ἀτιμίῃ τινὰ ἔχειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.3; ἀτιμίην προστιθέναι τινί [prev. author] 7.11; ὄνειδος καὶ ἀ. ἔχειν [prev. work] 231; ἀτιμίης κυρεῖν πρός τινος [prev. work] 158}}; θεῶν ἀ. **dishonour done to** the gods, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 72, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hipparchus” 229c; οὐκ ἀτιμίᾳ σέθεν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 796: _plural_, ταῖς μεγίσταις κολάζειν ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 309a, compare 310e, “R.” 492d, +others; ὕβρεις καὶ ἀτιμίας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.205, 21.23}}; **indignities,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1336b11.}}
**2** **deprivation of privileges,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 394 (Lyric poetry)}}; especially **of civic rights,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.74, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 9.6, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.44}}; coupled with θάνατος and φυγή, {{Refs|“IG” 1.27a74.}}
**II** of things, ἐσθημάτων ἀ., i.e. **sorry** garb, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 847; κόμη.. ἀτιμίας πλέως 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 9}}. [_Epic dialect_ ἀτιμῑη{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus [same place], 7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 10.10.]}}

820:
** ἄτῑμ-ος**, ον,
(τιμή{{Refs|1)}} **unhonoured, dishonoured,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.171}}; μετὰ πᾶσιν ἀτιμοτάτη θεός εἰμι{{Refs|[prev. work] 516; ἀτιμότερον δέ με θήσεις 16.90}}; ἀτιμότεροι, opposed to λαχόντες τιμῆς, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1111; ἄ. μόρος}}**dishonourable,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 589}}; ἄτιμα δ᾽ οὐκ ἐπραξάτην, i.e. they have met their deserts, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ag.” 1443}}; ἄτιμος Ἀργείοισι **by** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 440}}; ἔκ γ᾽ ἐμοῦ **by** me, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 51.}}
**b** c._genitive_, ἄ. δωμάτων **without the honour of.., not deemed worthy of**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 409 (Lyric poetry)}}; πάντων{{Refs|[prev. work] 295; ἐκφορᾶς [prev. author] “Th.” 1029}}; χάρις οὐκ ἄ. πόνων no **unworthy** return for.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ag.” 354; ὧν μὲν ἱκόμην ἄιμον ἐξέπεμψεν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 789.}}
**2** **deprived of civic rights** (compareἀτιμία), ἄτιμα τὰ τέκνα γίνεται{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.173, compare “IG” 1.37, 9(1).334 (_Locrian dialect_), etc.}}; opposed to ἐπίτιμος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 766, “Ra.” 692, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.80; ἄ. τὰ σώματα [prev. work] 74: with _genitive_; [prev. work] 75}}; ἄ. γερῶν **deprived of** privileges, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.58}}; ἄ. τοῦ τεθνηκότος **debarred from all rights in** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1214}}; ἄ. τοῦ συμβουλεύειν **deprived of the right of** advising, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.33; ἄ. τῆς πόλεως καθιστάναι τινά 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.21; ἄ. εἶναι καθάπαξ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.32, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 22.8.}}
**3** of things, **not honourable,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.6 (_superlative_)}}; ἄτιμον ποιεῖσθαί τι hold **in dishonour,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 78; ἄτιμα ποιεῖν ἔς τινα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.141; ἄ. τοὔργον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 166; ἕδρα ἀτιμοτέρα}}**less honourable,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.4.5}}; of parts of the body, τὸ τιμιώτερον καὶ τὸ ἀτιμότερον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 672b21; ἀ. σκεῦος 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.66.}}
**II** (τιμή{{Refs|11)}} **without price** or **value,** τοῦ νῦν οἶκον ἄτιμον ἔδεις thou devourest his substance **without payment,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.431}}; **of little price, cheap,** opposed to τίμιος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 4.10.}}
**II.2** **unavenged,** ἐκ θεῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1279, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1417.}}
**II.3** **unpunished,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 855c.}}
**III** _adverb_ **-μως** **dishonourably, ignominiously,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 197, 919, “Th.” 1026, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 428, variant in 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.17, etc.}}: _comparative_ **-ότερον**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 309b, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.67}}: _superlative_ ἀτιμότατα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 728b.}}

821:
** ἀτῑμ-όω**,
_future_ -ώσω: _aorist_ ἠτίμωσα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 644 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.: _perfect_ ἠτίμωκα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.103}}:—_passive_, _perfect_ ἠτίμωμαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 455, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.91}}: _pluperfect_ ἠτίμωτο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.231, “IG” 1.61a10}}: _aorist_ -ώθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 636 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.33, etc.}}: _future_ ἀτιμωθήσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.64; ἠτιμώσομαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.284}}:—**dishonour,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 644}}:—_passive_, **suffer dishonour** or **indignity,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.66, 7.231, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 636, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 455.}}
**II** **punish with** ἀτιμία{{Refs 5th c.BC+|2, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 742, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.33, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.82, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 53.6 (_passive_)}}; ἀτιμωθῆναι ἐπὶ αἰτίᾳ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.25; ἐκπεσόντα ἢ ἀτιμωθέντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 553b.}}

822:
** ἀτμ-ίς**, ίδος
(-ίτος[{{Refs 5th c.BC+|“PMag.Lond.” 1.121.639), ἡ, ={ἀτμός}, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.75, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 87a, 4th c.BC: Nicostratus Philosophus 15.5}}]: properly, **moist vapour, steam,** opposed to καπνός (but ἀτμὶς καπνοῦ{{LXX+4th c.BC+|LXX.Joel.2.30 (3.3)), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 359b30, compare 346b32, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.223, etc.; ἡ ἀ. συνίσταται εἰς ὕδωρ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 384a6.}}
**II** **sublimate** or **deposit** of colouring matter, {{Refs|“PHolm.” 4.21.}}
**III** **poultice,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Crito Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.879.}}
**IV** ={σπινθήρ, ἀπαύγασμα}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

823:
** ἄτομος**, ον,
**uncut, unmown,** λειμών{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 200; ἄ. πώγωνος βάθη 4th c.BC: Ephippus Comicus 14.7; ἄ. λίβανος}}**in lumps,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.68.1}}; ἄτομον, τό, ={ὑοσκύαμος}, uncertain in Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.68 (compare ἀταῖος).}}
**2** Grammars, of words, **not compound,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Thucydide” 22.}}
**II** **that cannot be cut, indivisible,** γραμμαί{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 206a17, compare “LI” 968a1; μεγέθη [prev. author] “Ph.” 187a3}}; especially of particles of matter, ἐτεῇ ἄτομα (i.e. σώματα) καὶ κενόν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 9, 125, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 404a2, “Metaphorical” 1039a10; in full, ἄ. σώματα [prev. author] “Cael.” 303a21, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 14 “fragment” 5: _sing._, ἄτομόν ἐστι σῶμα στερεόν.. [prev. author] p.129.24 U.; also ἄ. φύσεις 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 5, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.7U.; ἄτομοι, αἱ, [prev. work] p.14 U., +others, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 5, +others, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.34.}}
**II.2** of Time, οὐχ οἷόν τε εἰς ἀ. χρόνους διαιρεῖσθαι τὸν χρόνον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 263b27; κατ᾽ ἄ. χρόνον [prev. author] “Sens.” 447b18}}; ἐν ἀτόμῳ in a **moment,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 236a6, NT.1Cor.15.52; ἐν ἀ. ὀργῆς 2nd-3rd c.AD: Symmachus LXX.Isa.54.8.}}
**II.2.b** _metaphorically_, **infinitely small,** διαφοραί{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Phocion” 3.}}
**III** in Logic, **individual,** of terms, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 229d}}; of the εἶδος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1034a8, “de An.” 414b27.}}
**III.2** **individual,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “APo.” 96b11, +others:}}_substantive_ ἄτομον, τό,{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Cat.” 1b6, 3a38, “Metaphorical” 1058a18 (_plural_), 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.2.2, +others}}
**III.3** of the **summum genus,** πρὶν εἰς τὰ ἄ. ἐλθεῖν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 994b21}}. _adverb_ ἀτόμως, ὑπάρχειν **immediately, without the intervention of a middle term,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “APo.” 79a33.}}

824:
** ἄτοπ-ος**, ον,
**out of place, out of the way:** hence,
**1** **unwonted, extraordinary,** of symptoms, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 4.52: _comparative_, [prev. passage]}}; ἄ. ἁδονά{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 842 (Lyric poetry), compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1149a15; ὄρνις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 276; πόθος [prev. author] “Ec.” 956.}}
**2** **strange, paradoxical,** δοῦλοι τῶν αἰεὶ ἀτόπων slaves to every new **paradox,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.38; ἄτοπόν τι πάσχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.34; τῶν -ωτάτων μέντἂν εἴη 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.26}}; ἄτοπα τῆς σμικρολογίας **absurd** pettinesses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 175a; ἄ. ἡδονῆς καὶ λύπης μεῖξις [prev. author] “Phlb.” 49a}}; ἄτοπόν ἐστι, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 91, 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 125; οὐδὲν ἄ. εἰ ἀποθάνοιμι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 521d, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 11a37, +others, etc.}}
**2.b** of persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.149; ἄ. παιδευτής 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 493c; ἄ. καὶ δυσχερεῖς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.308; τὸν ἄτοπον φεύγειν ἀεί 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 203c}}; ἄ. φαγεῖν **given to strange** food, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.55.}}
**3** **unnatural, disgusting, foul,** πνεῦμα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49}}; **monstrous,** ἀτοπώτατον πρᾶγμα ἐξευρών{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.7}}; later, **wicked, wrong,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Job.27.6, NT.Luke.23.41}}; of persons, opposed to χρηστός, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 1}}; of things, **bad, harmful,** {{NT|NT.Acts.28.6.}}_adverb_ -πως **in an unfavourable position,** κεῖσθαι, of planets, Vett. Val.63.12.
**4** _adverb_ -πως **marvellously** or **absurdly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.30, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 95b, +others, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1136a12, etc.}}; ἀ. καθίζων, ={ἀνυπόπτως}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 180.}}
**II** **non-spatial,** τῆς ἰδέας μενούσης ἐν ἀτόπῳ αὐτὸ τόπους γεννῆσαν{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.5.8.}}_adverb_ -πως **non-spatially,** opposed to τοπικῶς, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Sententiae ad intelligibilia ducentes” 33.}}

826:
** αὐγάζω**,
_future_ -άσω: _aorist_ ηὔγᾰσα{{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.726 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus)}}:—_passive_, see below: (αὐγή):—**view in the clearest light, see distinctly, discern,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 217 (Lyric poetry), “Anthologia Graeca” 9.221 (Marc. argument); τὸν ἴδιον νοῦν οἷα πρὸς κάτοπτρον 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.156}}:—also in _middle_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.458, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 478, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.155, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 129, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.349 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus); αἰ δὲ λῇς αὐγάσδεο “Carmina Popularia” 18:}} — _passive_, αὐγασθεῖσα **being mirrored** in the smooth water, uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 598.6}}; simply, **appear,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC(?): Maximus Astrologus 11, +others, uncertain in 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 284.}}
**II** of the sun, **illumine,** τινά{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 637 (Lyric poetry)}}:—_passive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ba.” 596 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, **enlighten,** {{NT|NT.2Cor.4.4}}; **set in a clear light,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.659, +others}}
**III** _intransitive_, **appear bright** or **white,** {{LXX|LXX.Lev.13.25, +others}}; **shine,** {{Refs|PMag.Par. 1.2558, 2.143.}}

827:
** αὐγή**, ἡ,
**light of the sun,** and in _plural_, **rays, beams,** πέπτατο δ᾽ αὐ. ἠελίου{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.371, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.98, 12.176}}; ἠελίου ἴδεν αὐγάς, i. e. was born, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.188}}; ὑπ᾽ αὐγὰς ἠελίοιο, i.e. still alive, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.498, 619; Διὸς αὐγάς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.837}}; αὐγὰς ἐσιδεῖν see **the light,** i.e. to be alive, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 426, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 667; λεύσσειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 710; βλέπειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 935}}; ὑπ᾽ αὐγὰς λεύσσειν or ἰδεῖν τι hold up to **the light** and look at, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hec.” 1154, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 268a, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.3.1; ὑπ᾽ αὐγὰς δεικνύναι τι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 500 (πρὸς and ὑπ᾽ αὐγήν}} **in a full** and **in a side light,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 3)}}; δυθμαὶ αὐγῶν **sun**-set, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).65}}; ξύνορθρον αὐγαῖς dawning with **the sun,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 254 (Lyric poetry)}}; κλύζειν πρὸς αὐγάς rise surging towards **the sun,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 1182}}; λαμπροτάτη τῶν παρεουσέων αὐγέων brightest **light** available, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 3, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 658a3, “Pr.” 912b14, +others: _metaphorically_, βίου δύντος αὐγαί}} 'life's setting **sun**', {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1123 (Lyric poetry); ἤδη γὰρ αὑγὴ τῆς ζόης ἀπήμβλυνται 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 10.4.}}
**2** αὐγαὶ ἠελίοιο or αὐγαί alone, **the East,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 84, 231.}}
**3** **dawn, day-break,** {{NT|NT.Acts.20.11, “PLeid.W.” 11.35.}}
**4** generally, **any bright light,** πυρὸς αὐγή{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.305, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.456}}; ἀρίζηλοι δέ οἱ αὐγαί, of lightning,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|13.244; βροντῆς αὐ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1199 (Lyric poetry)}}; of a beacon, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.211, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 9; λαμπάδος 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus post 150}}; distinguished from φλόξ, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.186.}}
**5** of the eyes, ὀμμάτων αὐγαί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 70}}; αὐγαί alone, **the eyes,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1180 (Lyric poetry), “Rh.” 737: _metaphorically_, ἀνακλίναντας τὴν τῆς ψυχῆς αὐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 540a.}}
**6** **gleam, sheen,** of bright objects, αὐ. χαλκείη{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.341; χρυσὸς αὐγὰς ἔδειξεν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.83; ἀμβρόσιος αὐ. πέπλου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 983 (Lyric poetry); ἠλεκτροφαεῖς αὐ. [prev. author] “Hipp.” 741 (Lyric poetry); αὐ. τῆς κρόκης 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 561}}; of gems, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 2.8.—Mostly poetry, but frequently in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}}, chiefly in the sense of **sunlight.**

828:
** Αὔγουστος**, ὁ,
**Augustus,** used as an _adjective_, = Gr. σεβαστός, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 3.11.4}}, etc.:—hence Αὐγούστειος, ον, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 61.20}}: Αὐγουστεῖον, τό, **temple of Augustus,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 57.10}}: Αὐγούστεια, τά, **festival of A.,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 3.129, 14.739}}:—Αὐγουστάλιος, ον, **Augustalis,** τὰ Αὐγουστάλια **ludi Augustales,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 54.34}}; -άλιος, ὁ, **praefectus Augusti,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “Mag.” 2.3.}}
**II** the month **August,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Numa” 19.}}

829:
** αὐθάδ-ης**, ες,
**self-willed, stubborn,** ἦσάν τε αὐθαδέστεροι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.92; τὰς ὀργὰς αὐ. 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 24,compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1367a37}}; **surly,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 15.1; αὐθάδη φρονῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 907}}; of a dog, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.25.}}
**2** _metaphorically_ of things, **remorseless,** σφηνὸς γνάθος αὐ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 64.}}
**3** _adverb_ **-δως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1020, “POxy.” 1242.41}}: _comparative_ **-έστερον**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 34c}}; compare αὐτώδης. (From αὐτο-ἁδης,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Magna Moralia” 1192b33.)}}

830:
** αὐθ-αίρετος**, ον,
**self-chosen, self-elected,** στρατηγοί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.7.29}}; στεφανηφόρος **voluntary,** i.e. undertaking the duty at one's own expense, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Ath.Mitt.” 36.159 (Syros, 2nd c.AD),compare “IG” 12(5).660,668; γυμνασίαρχος “OGI” 583.8; συνήγορος “POxy.” 1242.10}}. _adverb_ **-τως**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Inscription Magn.” 163.15, “PLond.” 2.280.7 (1st c.AD).}}
**II** **by free choice, of one-self,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 931; αὐ. ἐξῆλθε NT.2Cor.8.17}}; **independent, free,** εὐβουλία{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.78; ἡ τοῦ τέλους ἔφεσις οὐκ αὐ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1114b6.}}
**III** of things, **due to one's own choice,** ὄλβος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus “fragment” 20}}; usually of evils, **self-incurred,** πημοναί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1231; οὐκ αὐθαίρετοι βροτοῖς ἔρωτες 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 339; νόσοι.. αἱ μέν εἰσ᾽ αὐ. [prev. work] 292.4}}; κίνδυνοι, δουλεία, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.144, 6.40; θάνατος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.2.36; λῦπαι 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 634; δυστύχημα [prev. author] 618}}. _adverb_ **-τως** **of free choice,** {{LXX+4th c.AD+|LXX.2Mac.6.19, +others, Mitteis “Chr.” 361 (4th c.AD)}}; πείθεσθαί τινι{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pelopidas” 24}}, **independently,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 34.}}

831:
** αὐθεντ-έω**,
**to have full power** or **authority over,** τινός I {{NT+1st c.BC+|NT.Tit.2.12; πρός τινα “BGU” 1208.37(1st c.BC)}}: with _infinitive_, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “Mag.” 3.42.}}
**2** **commit a murder,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Scholia 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 42.}}

832:
** αὐλέω**,
_Boeotian dialect_ _participle_ αὐλίων{{Refs|“IG” 7.3211,3212 (Orchom. _Boeotian dialect_)}}: (αὐλός):—**play on the flute,** Φρύγιον αὔλησεν μέλος{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 82, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.141, 2.60, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 327a: with _dative_ _person_, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.8, etc.}}; αὐ. ἔξοδον **play** a finale, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 582}}; αὐ. εἰρεσίαν, of the boatswain, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 2}}:— _passive_, of tunes, **to be played on the flute,** ὁ Βακχεῖος ῥυθμὸς ηὐλεῖτο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 9.3; αὐλεῖται πᾶν μέλαθρον}}**is filled with music,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 367.}}
**2** of persons, **play to,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 2.34, compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 302.1}}:—mostly in _passive_, **to be played to, hear music,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.11, “Cyr.” 4.5.7, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 917b19}} (but possibly _middle_ as in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 791a), 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 19.10, 20.10.}}
**II** generally, **play,** κέρατι{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 12.1, compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.74.}}

833:
** αὐλή**, ἡ,
**open court** before the house, **courtyard,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.433, 11.774, “SIG” 1044.17 (Halic., 4th-3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**2** **steading** for cattle, αὐλῆς ὑπεράλμενον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.138, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.5.}}
**II** later, **court** or **quadrangle,** round which the house was built, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.77, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 131, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 311a, etc.}}
**III** generally, **court, hall,** Ζηνὸς αὐ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.74, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.247; τὴν Διὸς αὐλήν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 122 (Lyric poetry); αὐ. νεκύων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 260 (Lyric poetry)}}; **court** of a temple, ἱεροῦ{{LXX+3rd c.BC+|“IG” 22.1299.28 (Eleusis, 3rd c.BC), compare [prev. work]1126.35, LXX.Psa.83(84).3}}; **any dwelling, abode, chamber,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 946 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.; of a cave, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 153 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἀγρόνομοι αὐλαί **homes** of dwellers in the wild, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 786 (Lyric poetry)}}; later, **country-house,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.50.}}
**IV** ἡ αὐλή **the Court,** αὐλὰς θεραπεύειν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 897, 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 97, “Comica Adespota” 145, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.26.9}}; οἱ περὶ τὴν αὐλήν the **courtiers,**{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work] 36.1, compare “OGI” 735.4 (2nd c.BC), “Inscription Mus.Alex.” 31; at Rome, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.10.3; ἡ βασίλειος αὐ. 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 3.11.7. (Wrongly explained as τόπος διαπνεόμενος (compare αὐλός) by 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 5.189b.)}}

834:
** αὐλ-ητής**, οῦ, ὁ,
**flute-player,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 941, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.141, 6.60, 129, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 581, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.12, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 327b, “OGI” 51.62 (3rd c.BC)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ αὐλειτάς {{Refs|“IG” 7.3195 (Orchom. _Boeotian dialect_).}}
**II** kind of **wasp,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**III** αὐ. ὑπονόμων sanitary **engineer,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Paraphrasis Ptolemaei Tetrabiblou” 250.}}

835:
** αὐλίζομαι**,
_aorist 1_ ηὐλισάμην always in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus, as 4.13, 6.7}}, compare (κατ-) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Timoleon” 12}}; ηὐλίσθην always in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus, as “An.” 4.1.11, +others; both in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, as 8.9}} (ἐν-), {{Refs|9.15: late}}_future_ αὐλισθήσομαι{{LXX|LXX.Tob.6.10}}: _perfect_ ηὔλισμαι{{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 3.29.7, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 1.17.5}}:—**lie in the** αὐλή or **courtyard,** μυκηθμοῦ.. βοῶν αὐλιζομενάων{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.265; κλαγγὴ.. συῶν αὐλιζομενάων 14.412}}; **take up one's abode, lodge, live** in a place, ἐν ἄντρῳ, of sheep, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.93}}; περὶ τὴν λίμνην, of birds,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|3.110, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 619a30; οἵοις ἐν πέπλοις αὐ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 304; ἄδειπνος.. ηὐλιζόμην}}**passed the night,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 322}}; especially as military term, **encamp, bivouac,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.9}}: in Medicine texts, of blood, **lodge** or **settle** in a place, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 2.2 (nisi to be read ἁλισθέν)}}: _metaphorically_, τὸ ἑσπέρας αὐλισθήσεται κλαυθμός{{LXX|LXX.Psa.29(30).6.}}
**II** _active_, **cause to dwell,**{{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Jer.38(31).9, 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 35.16.}}

836:
** αὐλός**, ὁ,
**pipe, flute, clarionet,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.13, 18.495, “hymnus ad Mercurium” 452; Λύδιος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 5.19}}; Ἔλυμος, i.e. Φρύγιος (which see), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 398; Λίβυς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 347}}; αὐ. γυναικήιος, ἀνδρήιος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.17}}; αὐ. ἀνδρεῖοι, παιδικοί, παρθένιοι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.176f, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.81; ὁ παρθένιος αὐ. τοῦ παιδικοῦ ὀξύτερος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 581b11; διδύμοις αὐλοῖσιν ἀεῖσαι 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus “Epigrammata” 5.1; ἐμφυσᾶν εἰς αὐλούς 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.59}}; αὐ. Ἐνυαλίου, i.e. **a trumpet,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca 6.151 (3rd c.BC: Tymnes Epigrammaticus)}}; ὑπ᾽ αὐλοῦ to the sound of **the flute,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}} [same place]; πρὸς τὸν αὐ., ὑπὸ τὸν αὐ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 6.3, etc.: _plural_, αὐλοὶ πηκτίδος}} **pipes** of the πηκτίς, {{Refs|“IG” 4.53 (from Aegina).}}
**2** **hollow tube, pipe, groove,** περόνη τέτυκτο αὐλοῖσιν διδύμοισι the buckle was furnished with two **pipes** or **grooves** (into which the tongue fitted), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.227}}; ἐγκέφαλος παρ᾽ αὐλὸν ἀνέδραμε spirted up beside **the vizard** (compare αὐλῶπις), or beside **the socket of the spear-head** into which the shaft fitted, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.297; but in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.18 αὐλὸς παχύς}} means the **jet** of blood **through the tube** of the nostril; αὐλὸς ἐκ χαλκείου the smith's **bellows,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 47, 77, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.100}}; **tube** of the clepsydra, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 914b14; βλέπειν δι᾽ αὐλοῦ [prev. author] “GA” 780b19.}}
**3** in animals, **blow-hole** of cetacea, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 589b19, “PA” 697a17}}; **funnel** of a cuttle-fish, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 524a10}}; **conus arteriosus** in fishes,{{Refs|[prev. work] 507a10, “Resp.” 478b8}}; **duct,** probably in {{Refs|[prev. author] “GC” 322a28.}}
**4** **stadium** (compare δίαυλος), {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 40.}}
**5** **haulm** of grain, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Scholia 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.46.}}
**6** **cow-bane, Cicuta virosa,** Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fluv.” 10.3.}}
**7** εἶδος ἀκολάστου σχήματος, {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 170.28.}}
**II** **razor-shell,** ={σωλήν} (which see), {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Diphilus Siphnius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.90d, 1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 32.103.}}

837:
** αὐξάνω**
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 153, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.16.ά, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 756, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 233, “fragment” 362.28, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 41d}}:—also αὔξω (_poetry_ ἀέξω, which see) {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 823, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 5.4, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 37, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 117 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 227, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 7.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 573a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.26, etc.}} (so _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. and Ptolemaic Papyrus; both forms in NT) _imperfect_ ηὔξανον only Ps.-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1132.25}}; ηὖξον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.31, etc.}}: _future_ αὐξήσω{{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.40, etc. (αὐξανῶ only in LXX.Gen.17.6, +others)}}: _aorist_ I ηὔξησα{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 11, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.24}}: _perfect_ ηὔξηκα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 90b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 2.15}}:—_passive_, αὐξάνομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.14, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 918, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1065, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.104, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 96c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.161; αὔξομαι 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 26.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 227, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 328d, etc.}}, _imperfect_ ηὔξετο[{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 493, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.39 (variant{αὔξετο})}}]: _perfect_ ηὔξημαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1248, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 371e}}, _Ionic dialect_ αὔξ-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.58}}: _pluperfect_ ηὔξητο{{Refs|[prev. author] 5.78}}: _aorist_ ηὐξήθην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.89, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 327c}}: _future_ αὐξηθήσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.48; αὐξήσομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.12, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 497a}}:—**increase,** not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (only ἀέξω), 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 153, etc.; ὕβριν αὐ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.16. ; ὄλβον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 756}}; opposed to ἰσχναίνειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 293b; εἰς ἄπειρον αὐ. τι [prev. author] “Lg.” 910b; ἐπὶ τὸ ἔσχατον [prev. author] “R.” 573a; ἐπὶ πλεῖον ηὖξον τὴν μαγειρικὴν τέχνην 3rd c.BC: Athenio Comicus 1.16, etc.}}
**2** **increase in power, strengthen,** αὐ. τὰ Ἑλλήνων **increase** their power, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.30; νόμοισιν αὐ. πόλιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 191, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.7.2}}; **exalt by one's deeds, glorify,** πόλιν, πάτραν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 5.4, “P.” 8.38, compare “IG” 22.834, etc.}}; **exalt by praise, extol,** ἐπαινεῖν καὶ αὔξειν τινά{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 206a}}; σέ γε.. καὶ τροφὸν καὶ ματέρ᾽ αὔξειν **honour** thee **as**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1092 (Lyric poetry)}}; of an orator, **amplify, exaggerate,** αὔξειν καὶ μειοῦν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1403a17.}}
**3** with an _adjective_, τρέφειν καὶ αὐ. τινὰ μέγαν **bring up** to manhood, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 565c; μείζω πόλιν αὐ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 572 (Lyric poetry); τὸν ὄγκον.. ἄπειρον αὐξήσει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 591d.}}
**4** αὔξειν ἔμπυρα **to sacrifice,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).62}}; compare ἀέξω.
**5** in Logic, ={καταπυκνόω} (which see), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 79a30, +others}}; but ὁ αὐξόμενος λόγος, name of a **fallacy,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 23, 2.559b.}}
**II** _passive_, **grow, increase,** in size, number, strength, power, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 493, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.93, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 61.5, etc.}}; αὐ. ἐς πλῆθος, ἐς ὕψος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.58, 2.14}}; of a child, **grow up,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.92}}.; ἐν γὰρ τοῖς πόνοισιν αὔξεται, of Theseus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 323}}; ηὐξανόμην ἀκούων I **grew taller** as I heard, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 638}}; of the wind, **rise,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.188; ηὔξηται ἡ πόλις ὥστ᾽ εἶναι τελέα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 371e.}}
**II.2** with an _adjective_, αὔξεσθαι μέγας **wax** great, **grow** up, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 183; αὐ. μείζων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 338, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 681a; αὐ. ἐλλόγιμος [prev. author] “Prt.” 327c; μέγας ἐκ μικροῦ.. ηὔξηται 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.21.}}
**III** later, _active_ _intransitive_, like _passive_, ἡ σελήνη αὐξάνει{{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 78b6, compare “HA” 620a21, Aristeas 208, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.64, NT.Col.2.19, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator Chr. 4.128, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 48.52, etc.}}
**IV** of Verbs, **take the augment,** both _active_ and _passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus “Epim.” 280}}; αὔξουσα (i.e. συλλαβή), ἡ, **augment,** {{Refs|[prev. passage] (Cf. ἀέξω.)}}

838:
** αὔξ-ησις**, εως, ἡ,
**growth, increase,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 6; τῶν ἐχθρῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.69, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Tiberius Gracchus” 13; κατὰ τὸ ποσόν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1069b11}}; of the Delta, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.13: in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus}}, **increments,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 546b}}; **multiplications,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 2.7.}}
**2** **amplification,** in Rhet., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1368a27, 1413b34; μετ᾽ αὐξήσεως ἐξαγγέλλειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.27.8, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Thucydide” 19 (_plural_).}}
**3** Grammars, **the augment,** {{Refs|EM 338.47.}}

839:
** αὔριον**,
_adverb_ **to-morrow,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.357, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.272, etc.}}; αὔ. τηνικάδε **tomorrow** at this time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 76b}}; ἐς or εἰς αὔ. **on the morrow,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.538}} (or **till morning,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.351), 4th c.BC: Nicochares Comicus 15, 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 4.4}}; **for the morrow,** καλέσαι ἐπὶ δεῖπνον εἰς αὔ.{{Refs|“IG” 22.17, etc.}}
**2** **presently, shortly,** φάγωμεν καὶ πίωμεν, αὔ. γὰρ ἀποθνῄσκομεν{{NT|NT.1Cor.15.32}}; opposed to σήμερον, {{NT|NT.Matt.6.30.}}
**II** _substantive_, αὔ. ἣν ἀρετὴν διαείσεται **the morrow** will distinguish.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.535.}}
**III** ἡ αὔ. (i.e. ἡμέρα) **the morrow,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 945 (“OT” 1090 is corrupt); τὴν αὔ. μέλλουσαν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 784; ἡ αὔ. ἡμέρα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 11.6, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 26.6; also ἡ ἐς αὔ. ἡμέρα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 567; τὸ ἐς αὔριον [prev. author] “fragment” 593.5; εἰς τὴν αὔ. 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 241.3, NT.Act.4.3}}; ἐπὶ τὴν αὔ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 5; ὁ αὔ. χρόνος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1117}}; ἡ Αὔ. personified by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 210 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus; δαίμονα τὸν Αὔριον 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 16}}. (Cf. Lithuanian _aušrà_ 'dawn', Sanskrit _usrás_ 'of the dawn'; see at {ἄγχαυρος},{ἕως}.)

840:
** αὐστηρός**, ά, όν,
(αὕω) **harsh, rough, bitter,** ὕδωρ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 61c, compare “Ti.” 65d}}; οἶνος αὐ., opposed to γλυκύς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 52, “Fract.” 29, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 872b35, 934a34; ὀσμή [prev. author] “de An.” 421a30}}; of country, **rugged,** τόποι{{Refs 1st c.BC+|“OGI” 168.57 (1st c.BC)}}: _metaphorically_, **harsh, crabbed,** ποιητής{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 398a (_comparative_)}}; **severe, unadorned,** ἡ πραγματεία ἔχει αὐ. τι{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.1.2, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 47; γυμνάδος αὐστηρὸν.. πόνον}}**severe,** {{Refs|epigram Gr. 201}}. _adverb_ -ρῶς, κατεσκευάσθαι{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 43.}}
**b** in moral sense, **rigorous, austere,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Eudemia” 1240a2; τοῖς βίοις 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.20.7 (_superlative_),compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ τοῦ καθ᾽ Ὅμηρον ἀγαθοῦ βασιλέως” p.23 O. (_comparative_)}}; αὐ. καὶ αὐθάδης{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.27, compare “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.162}}, Vett. Val.75.11; **strict, exacting,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Luke.19.21, “PTeb.” 315.19 (2nd c.AD)}}; αὐστηρότερον, τό, **excessive rigour**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 140.18 (2nd c.AD)}}. _adverb_ **-ρῶς**{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC(?): Satyrus Epigrammaticus “Vit.Eur. fragment” 39 iv 19:}} _comparative_ **-ότερον** {{LXX|LXX.2Mac.14.30.}}

841:
** αὐτάρκ-εια**, ἡ,
**self-sufficiency, independence,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 246, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 17, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 67a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1097b7, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.63U., etc.; αὐ. ζωῆς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1360b15; κτήσεως [prev. author] “Pol.” 1256b32; ἡ τῆς τροφῆς αὐ. [prev. author] “GA” 776b8.}}
**II** concrete, **a sufficiency,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PFlor. 242.8 (3rd c.AD)}}; **a competence,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 289.32.}}

842:
** αὐτάρκ-ης**, ες,
(ἀρκέω) **sufficient in oneself, self-supporting, independent** of others, ἀνθρώπου σῶμα ἓν οὐδὲν αὔ. ἐστιν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; αὐ. εἰς πάντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 271d}}; εἰς εὐδαιμονίαν, of ἀρετή, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.46; οὐκ αὐ. ἀλλὰ πολλῶν ἐνδεής 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 369b; ὁ σοφὸς -έστατος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1177b1, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 45; αὐτάρκη φρονεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 29; νηδὺς αὐ. τέκνων}}**helping itself, acting instinctively,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 757}}; αὐ. βοή a **self-reliant** shout, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1057 (assuming variant); πόλις αὐ. θέσιν κειμένη 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.37, compare 2.36 (_superlative_)}}; οἰκία -έστερον ἑνός, πόλις δ᾽ οἰκίας{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1261b11; τὸ τέλειον ἀγαθὸν αὔ. εἶναι δοκεῖ [prev. author] “EN” 1097b8}}; σῶμα αὔ. πρός τι **strong enough** for a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.51, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.8.11}}: with _infinitive_, **able of oneself** to do a thing, εἰ γὰρ αὐτάρκη τὰ ψηφίσματα ἦν ἢ ὑμᾶς ἀναγκάζειν κτλ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|D 3.14, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.3.4}}. _adverb_ -κως, ἔχειν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1362a27}}: _superlative_ -έστατα, ζῆν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.14.}}
**II** **sufficient** in quantity, ἀργύριον αὔ. εἰς σιτωνίαν{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.69; ὕδωρ αὔ. τοῖς ποιμνίοις 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 2.11.2, compare “PLond.” 3. 1166.6 (1st c.AD), “POxy.” 729.19 (2nd c.AD)}}; ὄξους τὸ -έστατον{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.1046.}}_adverb_ -κως **sufficiently**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“BGU” 665.18 (1st c.AD), 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.3.3, “Theologumena Arithmeticae” 45.}}

844:
** αὐτό-μᾰτος**, η, ον,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} and _Attic dialect_; ος, ον [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 103, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 762a9, {5-6th c.BC}: Philetaerus Comicus 1 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “EP” 19 in “Hermes” 53.65}}].
**1** of persons, **acting of one's own will, of oneself,** αὐ. δέ οἱ ἦλθε{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.408; αὐ. φοιτῶσι Νοῦσοι 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 103; αὐ. ἥκω 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1190, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.91, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.25, etc.}}
**2** of inanimate things, **self-acting, spontaneous,** of the gates of Olympus, αὐτόμαται δὲ πύλαι μύκον οὐρανοῦ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.749}}; of the tripods of Hephaistos, which ran of themselves, ὄφρα οἱ αὐτόματοι.. δυσαίατ᾽ ἀγῶνα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|18.376, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 188; ὅπλα.. αὐ. φανῆναι ἔξω προκείμενα τοῦ νηοῦ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.37; τὰ αὐ.}}**marionettes,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 734b10, 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Aut.” p}} assim: generally, **spontaneous,** βίος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 271e; ἔπαινος 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 64.}}
**3** of natural agencies, ὁ ποταμὸς αὐ. ἐπελθών **of itself,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.14}}; of plants, **growing of themselves,** αὐ. ἐκ τῆς γῆς γίνεται{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.100; αὐ. φύεσθαι [prev. author] 2.94, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 171.11; κύτισος αὐ. ἔρχεται 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 98.8: _metaphorically_, αὐτόματα πάντ᾽ ἀγαθὰ.. ποριζεται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 976, compare 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 160}}; of philosophers, αὐ. ἀναφύονται{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 180c.}}
**4** of events, **happening of themselves, without external agency,** αὐ. δεσμὰ διελύθη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 447}}; αὐ. θάνατος **natural** death, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.205; κόποι αὐ.}}**not to be accounted for externally,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 2.5; ἀπό τινος αἰτίας αὐτομάτης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 265c}}; **without visible cause, accidental,** opposed to ἀπὸ πείρης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.9.γ.}}
**II** αὐτόματον, τό, **accident,** τὸ αὐ. αἰτιᾶσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.25; σε ταὐ. ἀποσέσωκε 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 568; διὰ τὸ αὐ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 195b33; τὸ αὐ. ἀγαπῶντες [prev. author] “Ath.” 8.5}}; τῷ αὐ., opposed to τέχνῃ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1070a7: most frequently in the form ἀπὸ τοῦ αὐτομάτου or ἀπὸ ταὐτομάτου, ἀποθανέειν ἀπὸ τοῦ αὐ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.66, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.77, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 38c, +others, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1452a5, +others, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 31; ἐκ τοῦ αὐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.13}}; τὸ Αὐ. personified, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Ath.Mitt.” 35.458 (Pergam.); ταὐτόματόν ἐστιν ὡς ἔοικέ που θεός 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 291.}}
**III** _adverb_ -τως, ={ἀπὸ ταὐτομάτου}, variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.180, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 43, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 640a27, +others, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.27}}; **of itself,** κοχλίας αὐ. βαδίζων{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.13.11:}} —also αὐτοματεί or -τί (which see).

846:
** αὐτός**
(Cretan dialect ἀϝτός {{Refs|“GDI” 4976, +others), αὐτή, αὐτό}} (also αὐτόν{{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 3.4, +others)}}, reflexive _pronoun_, **self**:—in oblique cases used for the personal _pronoun_, **him, her, it**:—with _Article_, ὁ αὐτός, ἡ αὐτή, τὸ αὐτό (also ταὐτόν), etc., **the very one, the same.**
**I** **self, myself, thyself,** etc., _accusative_ to the person of the Verb: frequently joined with ἐγώ, σύ, etc. (see. below 10),
**I.1** **one's true self,** the **soul,** not the body, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.602}}; reversely, **body,** not soul, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.4}}; **oneself,** as opposed to others who are less prominent, as king to subject, {{Refs|6.18; Zeus to other gods, 8.4; bird to young, 2.317}}; man to wife and children, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.265}}; warrior to horses, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.466}}, or to weapons, {{Refs|1.47}}; shepherd to herd, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.167, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.51; Trojans to allies, 11.220}}; seamen to ships, {{Refs|7.338}}: generally, whole to parts,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 474; so later ἡ σίδη καὶ αὐτὴ καὶ τὰ φύλλα 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.10.7, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 1.19, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 511e, etc.; αὐτή τε Μανδάνη καὶ τὸν υἱὸν ἔχουσα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.1; αὐ. τε καὶ τὰ ποιήματα βουλόμενος ἐπιδεῖξαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 398a}}: _absolutely_, **the Master,** as in the {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus phrase Αὐτὸς ἔφα}} Latin _Ipse dixit_; so τίς οὗτος..;— Αὐτός, i.e. Socrates, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 218}}; ἀναβόησον Αὐτόν{{Refs|[prev. work] 219; ἀνοιγέτω τις δώματ᾽· Αὐτὸς ἔρχεται}}**the Master,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 268, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 314d, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 2.4, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 41: αὐ. ἀϋτεῖ 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.50: _neuter_, αὐτὸ σημανεῖ}} **the result** will show, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 623; αὐτὸ δηλώσει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.157; αὐτὰ δηλοῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 329b; αὐτὸ διδάξει [prev. work] 324a}}; especially αὐτὸ δείξει{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 177, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 288b, compare “Tht.” 200e; in full, τάχ᾽ αὐτὸ δείξει τοὔργον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 388; τοὔργον τάχ᾽ αὐτὸ δείξει 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 375; reduplicate, αὐτός θ᾽ ὁ χρήσας αὐτὸς ἦν ὁ μαρτυρῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 798}}; of things, **the very,** ὑπὸ λόφον αὐτόν, i.e. **just, exactly** under.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.615}}; πρὸς αὐταῖς ταῖς θύραις **close** by the door, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.12}}; αὐτὸ τὸ δέον the **very** thing needed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.7.7; αὐτὸ ὃ μάλιστα ἔδει ῥηθῆναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 362d}}; αὐτὸ τὸ περίορθρον **the point** of dawn, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.3}}; αὐτὰ τὰ ἐναντία the **very** opposite, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.5.7; αὐτὰ τὰ χρήσιμα καὶ ἀναγκαῖα 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Thucydide” 23}}; **even,** οὔ μοι μέλει ἄλγος οὔτ᾽ αὐτῆς Ἑκάβης{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.451; εἴ περ ἂν αὐταὶ Μοῦσαι ἀείδοιεν 2.597.}} —In these senses αὐτός in Prose either precedes both the _Article_ and _substantive_, or follows both, e.g. αὐτὸς ὁ υἱός or ὁ υἱὸς αὐτός. The _Article_ is sometimes omitted with proper names, or Nouns denoting individuals, αὐτὸς Μένων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.5; αὐτὸς βασιλεύς [prev. work] 1.7.11.}}
**I.2** **of oneself, of one's own accord,** ἀλλά τις αὐ. ἴτω{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.254; σπεύδοντα καὶ αὐτὸν ὀτρύνεις 8.293; καταπαύσομεν· οἱ δὲ καὶ αὐτοὶ παυέσθων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.168; ἥξει γὰρ αὐτά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 341}}; also, **in person,** τῶν πραγμάτων ὑμῖν.. αὐτοῖς ἀντιληπτέον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.2.}}
**I.3** **by oneself** or **itself, alone,** αὐτός περ ἐών although **alone,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.99}}; αὐτὸς ἐγείναο παῖδ᾽, i.e. **without a mother,**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5.880, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 924; ἀνακομισθῆναι αὐτὸν ἐς Φάληρον}}**by himself,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.85}}; αὐτοὶ γάρ ἐσμεν we are **by ourselves,** i.e. **among friends,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 504, compare “Th.” 472, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 137b, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.70, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.755c, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 10.2; αὐτοῖς τοῖς ἀνδράσι.. ἢ καὶ τοῖς ἄλλοις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.7; ἄνευ τοῦ σίτου τὸ ὄψον αὐτὸ ἐσθίειν [prev. author] “Mem.” 3.14.3; τὸν τρίβωνα ὃν αὐτὸν φορεῖ 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 22.13 (probably)}}; αὐτὰ γὰρ ἔστιν ταῦτα these **and no others,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 21.13, +others: strengthened, αὐτὸς κτήσατο οἶος}} **himself** alone, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.450}}; αὐτὸς μόνος, see at {μόνος} II; αὐτὸς καθ᾽ αὑτόν, see at {ἑαυτοῦ}.
**I.4** in Philosophy, **by** or **in itself,** of an abstract concept or idea, δίκαιον αὐτό{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 65d; αὐτὸ τὸ ἕν [prev. author] “Prm.” 143a, +others, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 997b8: _neuter_, αὐτό}} is frequently in this sense, attached to Nouns of all genders, οὐκ αὐτὸ δικαιοσύνην ἐπαινοῦντες ἀλλὰ τὰς ἀπ᾽ αὐτῆς εὐδοκιμήσεις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 363a; less frequently with _Article_, τί ποτ᾽ ἐστὶν αὐτὸ ἡ ἀρετή [prev. author] “Prt.” 360e}}; more fully, εἰ αὐτὸ τοῦτο πατέρα ἠρώτων, ἆρα ὁ πατήρ ἐστι πατήρ τινος, ἢ ο; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 199d}}; ἀδελφός, αὐτὸ τοῦτο ὅπερ ἔστιν **the ideal, abstract** brother, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. passage]e: later, in _composition_, αὐτοαγαθόν, αὐτοάνθρωπος, etc. (which see),compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1040b33}}; less frequently agreeing with the _substantive_, ἵνα αὐτὴ δικαιοσύνη πρὸς ἀδικίαν αὐτὴν κριθείη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 612c, etc.; doubled, ἐκ τῆς εἰκόνος μανθάνειν αὐτήν τε αὐτήν, εἰ καλῶς εἴκασται}}**its very self,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cra.” 439a.}}
**I.5** in _dative_ with _substantive_, **in one, together,** ἀνόρουσεν αὐτῇ σὺν φόρμιγγι he sprang up lyre **in hand,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.194}}; αὐτῇ σὺν πήληκι κάρη helmet **and all,**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|14.498, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.118; αὐτῷ σὺν ἄγγει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 32, compare “Hipp.” 1213; also without σύν, αὐτῇ κεν γαίῃ ἐρύσαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.24: so frequently in Prose and Poetry, αὐτοῖς ἀνδράσι}} men **and all,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.93}}; αὐτοῖσι συμμάχοισι allies **and all,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 223 (Lyric poetry); αὐτοῖς μελάθροις διακναιομένους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 164: with _Article_, αὐτοῖσι τοῖσι ἱματίοισι ἀπ᾽ ὦν ἔβαψε ἑωυτόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.47; αὐτοῖσι τοῖς πόρπαξι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 849, etc.; αὐτοῖς τοῖς ἵπποις κατακρημνισθῆναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.7.}}
**I.6** added to ordinal Numbers, e.g. πέμπτος αὐτός **himself** the fifth, i. e. **himself** with four others, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.46, compare 8.35, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.2.17, NT.Rev.17.11, etc.:—αὐτός always being the chief person.}}
**I.7** frequently coupled with οὗτος, τοῦτ᾽ αὐτό ἐστι τὸ ζητηθέν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 267c, etc.; αὐτὸ τοῦτο μόνον [prev. author] “Grg.” 500b; also λεγόντων ἄλλο μὲν οὐδέν.. αὐτὰ δὲ τάδε 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.139; πόλεις ἄλλας τε καὶ αὐτὸ τοῦτο τὸ Βυζάντιον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.1.27}}; ταῦτα ἥκω αὐτὰ ἵνα.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310e.}}
**I.8** καὶ αὐτός **himself** too, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.73, 14.45, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.5.9, etc.}}
**I.9** repeated in apodosi for emphasis, αὐτὸς ἐπαγγειλάμενος σώσειν.. αὐτὸς ἀπώλεσεν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.68, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 350, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.4.}}
**I.10** in connexion with the person. _pronoun_, ἐγὼν αὐτός{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.194; σέθεν αὐτοῦ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.312; νωΐτερον αὐτῶν 15.39 (always divisim in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}; followed by an _enclitic_ _pronoun_, αὐτόν μιν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.244; so αὐτὸν γάρ σε δεῖ Προμηθέως 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 86; also αὐτὸς ἔγωγε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59b, etc.:— after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus in the oblique cases αὐτός coalesces with the _pronoun_, ἐμαυτοῦ, σεαυτοῦ (these not in 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus or 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 80.10 ff.), ἑαυτοῦ, etc. (which see).}}
**I.10.b** with person. _pronoun_ omitted, αὐτός.. ἧσθαι λιλαίομαι, for ἐγὼ αὐτός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.252}}; αὐτὸν ἐλέησον, for ἐμὲ αὐτόν,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|24.503; αὐτῶν γὰρ ἀπωλόμεθ᾽ ἀφραδίῃσιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.27; in 2.33 οἱ αὐτῷ}} is simply a strengthened form of ο; and so in _Attic dialect_, when σὲ αὐτόν, ἐμοὶ αὐτῷ, etc., are read divisim, they are emphatic, not reflexive; in this case αὐτός generally precedes the person. _pronoun_,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.25 with 6.1.14.}}
**I.10.c** with the reflexive ἑαυτοῦ, αὑτοῦ, etc., to add force and definiteness, αὐτὸς καθ᾽ αὑτοῦ{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 406}}; αὐτοὶ ὑφ᾽ αὑτῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 194; αὐτοὶ καθ᾽ αὑτούς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.4; αὐτὸ καθ᾽ αὑτό 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 201e}}; sometimes between the _Article_ and _reflexive_ _pronoun_, τοῖς αὐτὸς αὑτοῦ πήμασιν βαρύνεται{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 836, compare “Pr.” 762; τούς γ᾽ αὐτὸς αὑτοῦ πολεμίους 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1132: also κατ᾽ αὐτὺ (_Boeotian dialect_ for αὐτοὶ) αὐτῶν “IG” 7.3172.121 (Orchom. _Boeotian dialect_).}}
**I.10.d** αὐτοῦ, αὐτῶν with _possessive_ _pronoun_, πατρὸς κλέος ἠδ᾽ ἐμὸν αὐτοῦ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.446; θρῆνον.. ἐμὸν τὸν αὐτῆς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1323; ἐχθρὸς ὢν τοῖς σοῖσιν αὐτοῦ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 416}}; τοῖς οἷσιν αὐτοῦ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1248; αὐτῶν σφετέρῃσιν ἀτασθαλίῃσιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.7; τοῖς ἡμετέροις αὐτῶν φίλοις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.1.29.}}
**I.10.e** αὐτὸς ἑαυτοῦ with _comparative_ and _superlative_ _adjective_, αὐτὸς ἑωυτοῦ ῥέει πολλῷ ὑποδεέστερος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.25; τῇ εὐρυτάτη ἐστὶ αὐτὴ ἑωυτῆς [prev. author] 1.203.}}
**I.11** αὐτός for ὁ αὐτός, **the same,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.225, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.107, 16.138, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.1 (never in Trag.)}}, and in later Prose, αὐταῖς ταῖς ἡμέραις{{NT+2nd c.AD+|“IG” 14.966 (2nd c.AD),cf. NT.Luke.23.12.}}
**I.12** _comparative_ αὐτότερος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 5}}: _superlative_ αὐτότατος **his very self,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 83: _neuter_ _plural_ αὐτότατα uncertain in 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 80}}. _adverb_, _comparative_ αὐτοτέρως{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).431.}}
**II** **he, she, it,** for the simple _pronoun_ of 3 _person_, only in oblique cases (except in later Gk., {{NT|NT.Luke.4.15, etc.)}}, and rarely first in a sentence, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 194e, and later, NT.Eph.2.10, etc.}}: rare in _Epic dialect_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.204 (where 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus treated it as enclitic)}}, and mostly emphatic,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 14.457, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.388}}; so in Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 421}}: in Prose, to recall a Noun used earlier in the sentence, ἐγὼ μὲν οὖν βασιλέα.. οὐκ οἶδα ὅ τι δεῖ αὐτὸν ὀμόσαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.4.7; πειράσομαι τῷ πάππῳ.. συμμαχεῖν αὐτῷ [prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.3.15; ἄνδρα δὴ.. εἰ ἀφίκοιτο εἰς τὴν πόλιν, προσκυνοῖμεν ἂν αὐτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 398a}}; after a Relative, ὅς κε θεοῖς ἐπιπείθηται.. ἔκλυον αὐτοῦ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.218; οὓς μὴ εὕρισκον, κενοτάφιον αὐτοῖς ἐποίησαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6. 4.9, compare 1.9.29}}; especially where a second Verb requires a change of case in the _pronoun_, οἳ ἂν ἐξελεγχθῶσι.. ὡς προδότας αὐτοὺς ὄντας τιμωρηθῆναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “An.” 2.5.27; ἐκεῖνοι οἷς οὐκ ἐχαρίζονθ᾽ οἱ λέγοντες οὐδ᾽ ἐφίλουν αὐτούς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.24; in subdivisions, ὅσοι.. οἱ μὲν αὐτῶν.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.1.1, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 168e}}; later, pleonastically after a Relative, ὧν ὁ μὲν αὐτῶν{{NT+5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 43, cf. NT.Luke.3.16, NT.Rev.7.2, etc.: in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 316 αὐτοῖς is emphatic 'in their own persons'.}}
**III** with _Article_ ὁ αὐτός, ἡ αὐτή, τὸ αὐτό, and _Attic dialect_ _contraction_ αὑτός, αὑτή, ταὐτό and ταὐτόν (as required by the metre,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 734 with 325, and in Prose to avoid hiatus): _genitive_ ταὐτοῦ, _dative_ ταὐτῷ}} _plural_ _neuter_ ταὐτ; _Ionic dialect_ ὡυτός, τὠυτό:—**the very one, the same,** rare in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.391, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.55, 326; ὁ αὐ. εἰμι τῇ γνώμῃ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.38, compare 5.75; ἐπὶ τὸ αὐ. αἱ γνῶμαι ἔφερον [prev. author] 1.79}}: with _dative_, to denote sameness or agreement, especially in Prose, τὠυτὸ ἂν ὑμῖν ἐπρήσσομεν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.119; τὸν αὐτὸν χῶρον ἐκλιπὼν ἐμοί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 543}}; ὁ αὐτὸς τῷ λίθῳ **the same as** the stone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 298a}}; ἐν ταὐτῷ εἶναί τινι to be **in the place with**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.27}}; προσίεσθαί τινα ἐς ταὐτὸ ἑαυτῷ to have a person **meet** one,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 30, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 210; κατὰ ταὐτὰ τῷ Νείλῳ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.20; τῇ αὐτῇ.. καί [prev. author] 4.109; τὴν αὐτὴν διάνοιαν καὶ κατ᾽ ἐκείνην ἡλικίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.83; ὁ αὐτός.. ὥσπερ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 86a; ἐς ταὐτὸν ἀλλήλοις}}**face to face,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.5a0.}}
**III.2** in later Greek, **the said, the above-named,** Ἡρώδης ὁ αὐ.{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PLille” 23.8 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**IV** Adverbial phrases:
**IV.1** αὐτὸ μόνον **simply, merely,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.252, etc.; αὐ. μόνον ἐργάτης 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Somn.” 9; αὐ. μόνον τὸ ὄνομα τῆς φωνῆς 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 22.20.}}
**IV.2** αὐτό as _adverb_, ={ἄρτι}, {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 540.1 (from Thrace).}}
**IV.3** αὐτὸ τοῦτο as _adverb_, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|“PGrenf.” 1.114 (2nd c.BC), NT.2Pet.1.5; τῆς αὐτὸ τοῦτο κινουμένης σφαίρας 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de communi mathematica scientia” 17.}}
**IV.4** with Preps., ἐπὶ τὸ αὐτό **added together, making a total,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PLond. 2.196.37 (2nd c.AD)}}; κατὰ τὸ αὐτό **together, at the same time,**{{NT|NT.Acts.14.1}}, etc.; but κατ᾽ αὐτό **just then,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.12.3.}}
**V** In Compos.:
**V.1** **of** or **by oneself, self-**.., as in αὐτοδίδακτος, αὐτογνώμων, αὐτόματος: and so, **independently,** as in αὐτοκράτωρ, αὐτόνομος.
**V.2** hence, **as a second self, very.., bodily,** as with proper names, Αὐτοθαΐς.
**V.3** **in the abstract, the ideal,** see above{{Refs|1.4.}}
**V.4** **precisely,** as in αὐτόδεκα.
**V.5** rarely with _reflexive_ sense of ἀλλήλων, as in αὐτοκτονέω.
**V.6** **in one piece with, together with,** as in αὐτόκωπος, αὐτοχείλης, αὐτόπρεμνος, αὐτόρριζος.
**V.7** **by itself**: hence, **only,** as in αὐτόξυλος, αὐτόποκος.—For αὐτοῦ, αὐτῶς, etc., see the respective Arts.

848:
** ἑαυτοῦ**, ῆς, οῦ, ἑαυτῷ, ῇ, ῷ, ἑαυτόν, ήν, ό,
_plural_ ἑαυτῶν, ἑαυτοῖς, ἑαυτούς άς, ά: _Ionic dialect_ **ἑωυτοῦ** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 57.44 (Milet., 5th c.BC), etc.}}; also ωὑτῆς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.84, ωὑτέου 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 1.7 (_Ionic dialect_ ἑωυ- by contraction of ἕο αὐ-, from which also _Attic dialect_ ἑᾱυ-, frequently written ἑατοῦ}} in Papyrus and Inscrr., as {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“SIG” 774.2 (Delph., 1st c.BC)}}: _Attic dialect_ _contraction_ **αὑτοῦ**, etc., which is the usual form in Trag., though ἑαυτοῦ, etc., are used (though rarely) when the metre requires, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 188 (_anapaest meter_), +others; in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. αὑτοῦ prevails after BC 300 4th c.BC}}; Cretan dialect **ϝιαυτοῦ** Kohler-Ziebarth **Stadtrecht von Gortyn** {{Refs|p.34}}; _Doric dialect_ **αὐταυτοῦ**, **αὐσαυτοῦ** (which see); Thess. **εὑτοῦ** (_dative_), {{Refs|“IG” 9(2).517.16}}: _genitive_ _plural_ **ηὑτῶν** {{Refs|“Schwyzer” 251 A 44 (from Cos)}}:—_reflexive_ _pronoun_ of _3rd pers._, **of himself, herself, itself**, etc.; first in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 78, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}}, and _Attic dialect_ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|(8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has ἕο αὐτοῦ, οἷ αὐτῷ, ἑ αὐτόν): αὐτὸ ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτό (variant -τοῦ)}} itself by **itself, absolutely**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 152b}}; αὐτὸ ἐφ᾽ αὑτοῦ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 160b; ὅταν τὸ ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτὸν ἕκαστος σπεύδῃ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.141; αὐτὸ καθ᾽ αὑτό 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 157a; αὐτὰ πρὸς αὑτά [prev. work] 154e}}; ἀφ᾽ ἑαυτῶν, ἑαυτοῦ, of **themselves, himself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.60, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.10.3}}; ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτοῦ, see at {ἐπ; ἐν ἑαυτῷ γίγνεσθαι}, ἐντὸς ἑαυτοῦ γ., see at {ἐν}, ἐντό; παρ᾽ ἑαυτῷ at **his own** house, {{Refs|[prev. work] 3.13.3, etc.: especially with _comparative_ and _superlative_, ἐγένοντο ἀμείνονες αὐτοὶ ἑωυτῶν they surpassed}}**themselves**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.86}}; πλουσιώτεροι ἑαυτῶν continually richer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.8; θαρραλεώτεροι αὐτοὶ ἑαυτῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 350a}}, compare d; τῇ αὐτὸ ἑωυτοῦ ἐστι μακρότατον at its very greatest length, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.8, compare 149, 4.85, 198.}}
**II** in _Attic dialect_, Trag., and later, αὑτοῦ, etc., is used for the _1st pers._ or _2nd pers._, as for ἐμαυτοῦ, αὐτὸς καθ᾽ αὑτοῦ τἄρα μηχανορραφῶ [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 221, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 138, etc.; for σεαυτοῦ, μόρον τὸν αὑτῆς οἶσθα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1297, compare 1141, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 101c (variant), 3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 59.16, etc.: so in _plural_, τὰ αὑτῶν (={ἡμῶν αὐτῶν}) ἐκποριζώμεθα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.82; δώσομεν ἑαυτούς 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 47}}]; ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτοῖς by **ourselves**, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.1Ki.14.9, compare “PPar.” 47.26 (2nd c.BC), NT.2Cor.7.1, etc.}}; ἑαυτῶν, ={ὑμῶν αὐτῶν}, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PPar.” 63.128 (2nd c.BC).}}
**III** _plural_, ἑαυτῶν, ἑαυτοῖς, etc., is sometimes used for ἀλλήλων, ἀλλήλοις, **one another**, διάφοροι ἑωυτοῖσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.49; παρακελευόμενοι ἐν ἑαυτοῖς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.25, etc.}}; καθ᾽ αὑτοῖν **one** against **the other**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 144 (_anapaest meter_); πρὸς αὑτούς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.19; περιιόντες αὑτῶν πυνθάνονται [prev. author] 4.10, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 215b.}}

849:
** αὐτό-χειρ**, χειρος, ὁ, ἡ,
**with one's own hand, creative,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 592 (Lyric poetry)}}; αὐ. λούειν, παίειν, κτείνειν, [{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 900, 1315, “Aj.” 57; τίνες ᾠκοδόμησα;—ὄρνιθες αὐτόχειρες 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1132 {αὐτοχειρί}, compare “Lys.” 269, 5th c.BC: Theopompus Comicus 86, NT.Act.27.19, etc.}}]: with _genitive_, **very doer, per-petrator of** a thing, αὐ. τοῦδε τοῦ τάφου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 306}}; τῆς ἀσελγείας ταύτης{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.60}}; αὐ. οὔτε τῶν ἀγαθῶν οὔτε τῶν κακῶν **men who accomplish** neither.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.150.}}
**II** _absolutely_, **one who kills himself, suicide,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1175; αὐ. ἑαυτῶν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 502}}; simply, **murderer,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 231, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.116}}; αὐτὸν.. νομίζω αὐτόχειρά μου γεγενῆσθαι τούτοις τοῖς ἔργοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]106: with _genitive_, αὐ. καὶ φονεῖς τῶν πολιτῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.111, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 216; in full, τὸν αὐ. τοῦ φόνου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 266, compare “El.” 955, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.287.}}
**III** as _adjective_, **murderous,** especially of murder **committed by one's own hand** or **by kinsmen,** αὐ. θάνατος, σφαγή, μοῖρα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 880, “Or.” 947, “Med.” 1281 (Lyric poetry)}}; πληγέντες αὐτόχειρι σὺν μιάσματι, of brothers smitten by **mutual slaughter,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 172; φόνος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 615c.}}
**III.2** αὐτόχειρα γράμματα **written with one's own hand, autograph,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 59.4.}}

850:
** αὐχμηρός**, ά, όν,
**dry, without rain,** χειμών{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 3.11, compare “Aër.” 10; ἔτη 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 605b19; ἔαρ [prev. author] “Pr.” 860a13}}; of places, **dry, parched,** τόποι{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 761b (_superlative_),cf. NT.2Pet.1.19; χωρία 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.11.10, etc.; καρποί 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.53.}}
**b** **parching,** νόσοι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 121.3.}}
**2** **dry, rough, squalid,** οὖδας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 947; σκληρὸς καὶ αὐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 203d}}; especially of hair (compare {αὐχμηροκόμης}), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 475, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 387, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.225; βίος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 1}}. _adverb_ -ρῶς, ἔχειν τοῦ προσώπου{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 4.10.}}
**3** **miserable,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 2.169: with _genitive_, βιότοιο [prev. work] 454: irregular _superlative_ αὐχμότατος}} uncertain reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 169.}}

851:
** ἀφαιρ-έω**,
_Ionic dialect_ ἀπαιρέω, _future_ -ήσω: _perfect_ ἀφῄρηκα, _Ionic dialect_ ἀπαραίρηκα: _aorist_ ἀφεῖλον, later _infinitive_ ἀφέλαι{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“GDI” 4940.35 (Cretan dialect); ἀφῄρησα 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.121}}:—**take away from**:—Constr.: mostly ἀ. τί τινι, σῖτον μέν σφιν ἀφεῖλε **took** it **from** him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.455, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 360}} codices, etc. (but also, **relieve** one **of** a duty, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.44): less frequently ἀ. τί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 560, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 4.7; κῆρα χώρας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 777 (Lyric poetry); ἔκ τινος [prev. author] “Eu.” 444}}; also τινά τι probably {{Refs 5th c.BC+|l. [prev. work] 360, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 933}}, see infr. II. I, III: with _genitive_, **take from,** τιμῆς οὔτ᾽ ἀφελὼν οὔτ᾽ ἐπορεξάμενος{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 5.2}}; μηδὲν ἀφαιρῶμεν τοῦ ἀδίκου (**from** the unjust man) ἀπὸ τῆς ἀδικίας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 360e}}; τοῦ πλήθους **diminish** the number, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 4.4: with _accusative_ only, ἀπελὼν τὰ ἄχθεα}} **having taken** them **off,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80; βασιλέων.. ὀργὰς ἀφῄρουν}}**took away,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 455, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 22, “Ra.” 518.}}
**2** **set aside,** κρέα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 1044.41 (Halic., 4th c.BC).}}
**2.b** **exclude, separate,** τὸ Ἑλληνικὸν ὡς ἓν ἀπὸ πάντων ἀφαιροῦντες χωρίς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 262d}}; opposed to προστιθέναι, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 95e, etc.}}
**3** in Mathematics texts, ἀ. ἀπὸ.. **subtract** from, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra “Ax.” 3 (_passive_)}}, etc.; of ratios, **divide out** from both sides of an equation, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus Perg. 1.41 (_passive_)}}; **intercept,** in _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Hypotyposis astronomicarum positionum” 2.27.}}
**4** in Logic, **abstract,** with _accusative_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 74a37, +others: _absolutely_, [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1030a33.}}
**II** _middle_, _future_ ἀφαιρήσομαι (in _passive_ sense, variant for{ἀπαιρε-θήσεσθαι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.35, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator “fragment” 57), later ἀφελοῦμαι 2nd c.BC: Timostratus Comicus 5, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.29.7}}: _aorist_ ἀφειλόμην, later ἀφειλάμην {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.586, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 41.63, compare 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista}} 116: _perfect_ ἀφῄρημαι (in _middle_ sense) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.79 (spelt ἀφείρ- “Sammelb.” 4309 (3rd c.BC)):—from 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} downwards more frequently than _active_, **take away for oneself**; also in reciprocal sense, ἀφαιρεῖσθον τύχην ye **have received each** the fortune **of the other,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 928:}} —Constr. like _active_, ἀφαιρεῖσθαί τί τινι, as καὶ δή μοι γέρας.. ἀφαιρήσεσθαι ἀπειλεῖς{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.161; τί τινος 5.673, 691, 9.335, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.58, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.13, etc. (also τεύχεα.. ὤμοιϊν ἀφελέσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.510); τι πρός τινος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1034; τι ἀπό τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 883; ἔκ τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 12.9}}: with _double_ _accusative_ _of things_ et _person_, **bereave** or **deprive of,** μήτε σὺ τόνδ᾽.. ἀποαίρεο κούρην{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.275, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.71, 7.104}}; frequently in _Attic dialect_ and Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator [same place], 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.74, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.46, etc.; τέκνα ἀ. τινά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 613, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 464: rarely with _accusative_ _person_ et _genitive_ _of things_, ἀ. τὰς κύνας τοῦ εὑρεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.4; τῆς ἀρχῆς τινά 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 60; τὴν Ἀμαζόνα τοῦ ζωστῆρος 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.10.9.}}
**II.2** with _accusative_ _of things_, ἀ. ψήφισμα **cancel** or **rescind,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.24}}; ἀφελομένης τῆς νυκτὸς τὸ ἔργον **having broken off** the action, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.134; ἕως κελαινῆς νυκτὸς ὄμμ᾽ ἀφείλετο 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 428: _absolutely_, μέχρι σκότος ἀφείλετο (i.e. τὴν δίωξιν) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.2.16; ἀ. τὴν μνήμην πολλῶν ἀγαθῶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.13.}}
**II.3** followed by μή with _infinitive_, **prevent, hinder from** doing, τί μ᾽ ἄνδρα.. ἀφείλου μὴ κτανεῖ;{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1303, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1146}}; κἄκτεινας, ἤ τις συμφορά σ᾽ ἀφείλετο [μὴ κτεῖναι]**;** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Andr.” 913}}; with _infinitive_ _passive_, τὸν τὰ ὕστερον ἀφείλετο ἀδικήματα εὐεργέτην μὴ ὀνομασθῆναι{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 8.52.2}}; with _infinitive_ only, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.62}}: simply, **obstruct,** ἀρχήν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 958c.}}
**II.4** ἀ. τινὰ εἰς ἐλευθερίαν, Latin _vindicare in libertatem, claim_ as free, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 914e, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.97, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 58.19, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 23.10, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.62.}}
**III** _passive_, _future_ **-αιρεθήσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 938}} **-ήσομαι** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator “fragment” 57:}}_perfect_ ἀφῄρημαι, _Ionic dialect_ ἀπαραίρημαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.159, etc.}}:—**to be robbed** or **deprived of** a thing, τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 962 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.137, etc.}}; τι πρός or ὑπό τινος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.70, 3.65, 7.159; ἀφῃρέθην τὰ ἐνέχυρα ὑπό τινος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.41}}; ἐκ χερῶν ἀφῃρέθην **had** them **taken** out of my hands, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 486: with}}_infinitive_, ἀφῃρέθη Σκίρωνος ἀκτὰς ὄμμα τοὐμὸν εἰσορᾶν **was deprived of, hindered from** seeing them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hipp.” 1207: less frequently μηδὲν τοῦ ἐμοῦ ὄγκου ἀφαιρεθέντος ἀλλὰ σοῦ αὐξηθέντος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 155b.}}
**III.2** ὁ ἀφαιρεθείς, in Law, **the person from whom** a slave **has been claimed,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 915a.}}

852:
** ἀφᾰν-ής**, ές,
(φαίνομαι) **unseen,** especially of the nether world, Ταρτάρου πυθμήν{{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 207, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 860 (Lyric poetry); ἀ. κἀν Ἀΐδα δόμῳ φοιτάσῃς 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 68}}; χάσμα ἀ. a **blind** pit, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.76}}; ἡ ἀ. θεός, of Persephone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1556 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὁ ἀ. πόλος, i. e. the south pole, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 285b21, “Mu.” 394b31 (but ἀ. κόσμος}}**starless,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 6.22).}}
**2** ἀ. γίγνεσθαι, ={ἀφανίζεσθαι}, **disappear,** ὑπὸ γῆν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.104, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 757, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 360a; so ἀ. ἦν}}**disappeared,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.37, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.7}}; of soldiers **missing** after a battle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.34}}; **runaway, absconded,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PGen. 5.4(2nd c.AD).}}
**2.b** στήλας ἀ. ποιῆσαι **obliterate**, {{Refs|“SIG” 38.38 (from Teos).}}
**3** **unnoticed, secret,** ἀ. νόος ἀθανἁτων{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 17}}; ἀ. νεῦμα a **secret** sign, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.134}}; ἀ. χωρίον **out of sight,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.29, compare [prev. work] 67}}; ἀ. ξιφίδιον **concealed,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.69}}; δι᾽ ἐπιστολῶν ἀφανῶν **secret** or **invisible** writings, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 102.29: with}}_participle_, ἀ. εἶναι ἀπιόντες depart **without being noticed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.2.4; ἀ. ὄντες ἠδίκουν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.68}}; μαντικῇ χρώμενος οὐκ ἀ. ἦν he was **well known to** do.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.2.}}
**3.b** **uncertain, doubtful,** ἀ. νοῦσοι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.84}}; σὺν ἀφανεῖ λόγῳ on an **uncertain** charge, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 657 (Lyric poetry); ἐν ἀφανεῖ λ. 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.59; μόρος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1682 (Lyric poetry); ὄνομα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1322 (Lyric poetry); ἐλπίς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.103; πρόφασις ἀφανεστάτη λόγῳ [prev. author] 1.23; οὐκ ἀ. τεκμήρια 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 6.1}}; μεθέντας τἀφανῆ, opposed to τὸ πρὸς ποσί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 131}}; ἀ. χάρις a favour **from an unknown hand,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.240; ἐς ἀφανὲς τὸν μῦθον ἀνενείκας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.23; μισῶ μὲν ὅστις τἀφανῆ περισκοπῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 737; τὰ ἀ. μεριμνᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 672; ὑπὲρ τῶν ἀ. φανεροῖς μαρτυρίοις χρῆσθαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1104a13}}; of what is **beyond the evidence of sense,** opposed to φανερόν, ἁρμονίη ἀ. φανερῆς κρείττων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 54, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 1, +others; τἀφανὲς διὰ τοῦ φαινομένου συλλογίζεσθαι 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 14.4; τὸ τῆς τύχης ἀ. οἷ προβήσεται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 785; τὸ ἀ. τοῦ κατορθώσειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.42}}; ἐν ἀφανεῖ ἔτι κεῖσθαι, ἐν τῷ ἀ. εἶναι, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.42, 3.23; ἐν ἀ. κεκτῆσθαί τι}}**secretly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 954e; ἐκ τοῦ ἀφανοῦς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.51, 4.96, etc.; ἐξ ἀ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 57.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1332}}: _neuter_ _plural_ as _adverb_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1289 (Lyric poetry)}}. Regul._adverb_ ἀφανῶς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.43, etc.}}: _superlative_ **-έστατα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.27.}}
**4** of persons and things, **unnoticed, obscure,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1244; also οὐ γὰρ ἀ. κρινεῖτε τὴν δίκην 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.57; ἀ. καὶ ταπεινὴ φύσις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 61.35.}}
**5** ἀ. οὐσία **personal** property, as money, **which can be secreted and made away with** {{Refs|compare (ἀφανίζω 1.7), opposed to φανερά}} (**real**), as land, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.4, compare “BCH” 27.219 (from Crete)}}; opposed to ἐμφανής, {{Refs|“IG” 12(2).15.8 (Mytil.), “SIG” 554.17}}; but simply, **concealed,** ἀφανῆ καταστῆσαι τὴν οὐσίαν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.23: in literal sense, ἀ. πλοῦτος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 602; πλοῦτος ἀ. ὃν σὺ κατορύξας ἔχεις 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 128.16.}}

853:
** ἀφᾰν-ίζω**,
_Attic dialect_ _future_ **-ῐῶ**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 184a}}: _perfect_ ἠφάνικα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.18}}:—**make unseen, hide,** νεφέλη.. ἠφάνισεν ἥλιον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.8}}; **hush up,** ἔργον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 217e}}: hence, **lose sight of,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 107.18; ἀ. τὸ συμφορώτατον}}**do away with, reject,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 21 (variant for{ἀφαιρέοντας})}}]; **make away with** a person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.126, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.53, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.80; μή μ᾽ ἀφανίσῃ λαβών 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 210:}} —_passive_, τὴν γνώμην μηδὲν.. ἀφανισθεῖσαν in no part **concealed** or **suppressed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.8.}}
**2** **do away with, remove,** ἄχος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1712 (Lyric poetry)}}; τινὰ πόλεος **carry** one **off from** the city, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1041 (Lyric poetry); Μούσας ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 972}}; ἀ. αὑτὸν εἰς τὸν νεών **disappear** into the temple, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pl.” 741.}}
**3** **destroy,** Ἀθήνας{{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.11, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.81.6, LXX.Deu.7.2; ὅλως ἀ. ἱερά 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.147, compare “epigram Gr.” 376.8 (from Aezani).}}
**4** **obliterate** writing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.54; footsteps, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.3, etc.}}; traces of bloodshed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.45}}; **spirit away** a witness, {{Refs|[prev. work]52}}; **get rid of,** δίκην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 760.}}
**5** **secrete, steal,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 14.2.}}
**6** **obscure, mar** one's good name, etc., πατρικὰς ἀρετάς, ἀξίωσιν, δόξαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.69, 2.61: in good sense, ἀ. ἀγαθῷ κακόν}} **wipe out** ill deeds by good,{{Refs|[prev. work] 42; δύσκλειαν [prev. author] 3.58}}; τὰ χρώματα ἀ. ἐκ τοῦ σώματος, of the **wasting** effect of grief, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 98}}; τρίχα βαφῇ ἀ. **disguise** it by dyeing, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 7.20}}; ἀ. τὰ πρόσωπα (compare ἀπρόσωπος), of **artificial disfigurement,** {{LXX+NT|NT.Matt.6.16, cf. LXX.Joel.2.20, LXX.Zech.7.14.}}
**6.b** **spoil,** οἶνον, ὕδωρ, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.90, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 9.645.}}
**7** **make away with** property, etc., ἀργύριον, ναυτικόν, ἀνθρώπους, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.101, 3.222, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.12; ἀ. τὴν οὐσίαν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.103}}; but, **conceal the existence of,** ἐργαστήριον, οὐσίαν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.26,44.}}
**8** **drain** a cup of wine, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 82.}}
**9** ἀφανίσαι· σκεπάσαι, προνομεῦσαι, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II** _passive_, **disappear, be missing,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.8, 124, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 255}}; of persons **buried** by a sand-storm, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.26}}; or **lost** at sea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.38, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.6.33}}; ἀ. κατὰ τῆς θαλάσσης, of islands, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.6; ὑποβρύχιος ἠφ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Crassus” 19; ἀ. ἐκ τῶν Θρηίκων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.95; ἐξ ἀνθρώπων 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.11}}; ἀ. εἰς ὕλην **disappear** into it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 10.23}}; καταγελασθὲν ἠφανίσθη was laughed down and **disappeared,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.83.}}
**II.2** **live retired,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 9.1.}}

854:
** ἀφᾰν-ισμός**, ὁ,
**extermination, destruction,** {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.11.5; πόλεων 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.48; σώματος καὶ ψυχῆς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.107d; ἀφανισμῷ ἀφανίζειν LXX.Deu.7.2.}}
**II** =[{{Refs 4th c.BC+|{ἀφάνισις} 11, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 580b21, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 19; ἄστρων 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 2 (_plural_), compare 2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 2.5}}]; **occultation,** {{Refs|Theo Sm. p.137}} H.; Περὶ ἀ. ἡλιακῶν, title of work by Eudoxus, on **occultations** of stars by the sun, {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 1.21; τῆς σελήνης 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.670c; ἐγγὺς ἀφανισμοῦ NT.Heb.8.13.}}

855:
** ἄφαντ-ος**, ον,
(φαίνομαι) **made invisible, blotted out,** ἀκήδεστοι καὶ ἄ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.60; ἄσπερμος γενεὴ καὶ ἄ. ὄληται 20.303, etc.}}; **hidden,** ἄ. ἕρμα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1007 (Lyric poetry); ἔφην᾽ ἄφαντον φῶς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 297; ἄ. ἔπελες 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.46; ἐκ βροτῶν ἄ. βῆναι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 832}}; ἁνὴρ ἄ. ἐκ.. στρατοῦ he has **disappeared,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 624}}; ἄ. οἴχεσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 657, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.59a; ἔρρειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 560; ἀρθεῖσ᾽ ἄ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 606; ἐκ χερῶν [prev. author] “Hipp.” 827 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἴχνος πλατᾶν ἄ. **disappearing,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 695 (Lyric poetry)}}; **invisible,** νύξ{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 9.3.}}
**2** **in secret,** ἄφαντα βρέμειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.30.}}
**3** **obscure,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “N.” 8.34}}; θεοῖς δῆλος θνητοῖσι δ᾽ ἄ. Epimenid.II.—poetical and late Prose, ἄ. γενέσθαι{{NT+4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.60, 4.65, NT.Luke.24.31; τὰ ἄφαντα φήναντες 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.260 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, cf. Scholia 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 899.}}

857:
** ἀφειδ-ία**, ἡ,
**generosity, liberality,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Definitiones” 412c, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.762d.}}
**2** **unsparing treatment,** σώματος{{NT|NT.Col.2.23.}}

859:
** ἄφεσις**, εως, ἡ,
(ἀφίημι) **letting go, release,** περὶ τῆς τῶν πλοίων ἀφέσεως Philipp. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.77, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 273c; καρπῶν “PAmh.” 2.43.9 (2nd c.BC)}}; γῆ ἐν ἀφέσει land **in private hands,** opposed to βασιλική, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 5.37 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**b** of persons, **dismissal**: in ritual, λαοῖς ἄ.{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Apuleius Scriptor Botanicus “Met.” 11.17}}; **release,** {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.79.12, “IG” 2.314.21, NT.Luke.4.18.}}
**2** with _genitive_, ἀ. φόνου **quittance from** murder, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 869d: so _absolutely_, 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ τῶν στάσεων” 8}}; **discharge from** a bond, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.3; ἄ. ἐναντίον μαρτύρων ποιήσασθαι [prev. author] 45.41}}; opposed to ἀπόδοσις χρημάτων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.29}}; **exemption from attendance, leave of absence,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 30.6}}; ἀ. τῆς στρατείας **exemption from** service, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agesilaus” 24}}; **remission** of a debt, ταλάντου{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Michel” 1340B7 (Cnidus, 2nd c.BC)}}; χρημάτων{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IPE” 12.32 B 70 (Olbia, 3rd c.BC)}}; i.e. καταδίκης, {{Refs|“Inscription Magn.” 93c16.}}
**2.b** **forgiveness, ** NT.Mark.3.29; ἁμαρτιῶν{{NT|NT.Matt.26.28.}}
**3** **relaxation, exhaustion,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 3.6.}}
**4** **divorce,** τινὶ πέμπειν{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 42.}}
**5** **starting** of horses in a race, ἵππων ἄ. ποιεῖν{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.73}}: hence, **starting-post** itself, ἰσώσας τἀφέσει (Musgr. for τῇ φύσει) τὰ τέρματα having made the winning-post one with the **starting-post,** i.e. having completed the δίαυλος and come back to the **starting-post,** uncertain conjecture in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 686, compare 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.15.5, 6.20.9}}: _metaphorically_, **the first start, beginning** of anything, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 3.405, etc.}}
**6** **discharge, emission,** ὕδατος{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 697a24; βέλους 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.41; τοῦ θοροῦ, τοῦ ᾠοῦ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 756a12; τοῦ κυήματος [prev. author] “HA” 608a1}}; **the dropping** of a foal, {{Refs|[prev. work] 576a25.}}
**6.b** **discharge, release** of an engine, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 58.24.}}
**7** ={ἀφεσμός}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 625a20 (_plural_).}}
**8** **release,** ὕδατος{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.34 (3rd c.BC)}}: hence, in concrete sense, **conduit, sluice,**{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. work] 3p.88, “PFlor.” 388.44 (3rd c.AD): _plural_, ἀφέσεις θαλάσσης}}**channels,** {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.22.16.}}
**9** Astrology texts, **reckoning of the vital quadrant,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 127, compare 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 136.2 (but ἀπὸ Λέοντος τὴν ἄφεσιν ποιούμενοι}} simply, **starting from**.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 31.8).}}

860:
** ἁφή**, ἡ,
(ἅπτω) **lighting, kindling,** περὶ λύχνων ἁφάς about lamp-**lighting** time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.215, compare “PTeb.” 88.12 (2nd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 11.33, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.31, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 12.526c.}}
**II** (ἅπτομαι) **touch,** ἐπώνυμον δὲ τῶν Διὸς γέννημ᾽ ἁφῶν (Wieseler for γεννημάτων) τέξεις.. Ἔπαφον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 850.}}
**II.2** **sense of touch,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 523e, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1118b1, “de An.” 424a12; ἀκριβεστάτην.. τῶν αἰσθήσεων τὴν ἁφήν [prev. author] “HA” 494b16; ἡ ἁφὴ ἐν ταῖς αἰσθήσεσι παρέσπαρται 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 70.}}
**II.3** **touch of the harp-strings,** _metaphorically_, ἐμμελοῦς ἁφῆς καὶ κρούσεως{{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 15; οὐχὶ συμφώνους ἁφάς 4th-3rd c.BC: Damoxenus Comicus 2.42.}}
**II.4** **grip,** in wrestling, etc., ἁφὴν ἐνδιδόναι αὑτοῦ{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.86f: _metaphorically_, τοῖς ἀφληταῖς τῆς λέξεως ἰσχυρὰς τὰς ἁ. προσεῖναι δεῖ καὶ ἀφύκτους τὰς λαβάς 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 18; ἁφὰς ἔχει καὶ τόνους ἰσχυρούς [prev. author] “Lys.” 13}}; ἁ. εἶχεν ἡ συνδιαίτησις ἄφυκτον, of {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Cleopatra, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 27.}}
**II.5** **sand sprinkled over wrestlers, to enable them to get a grip of one another,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.15.4; ἁφῇ πηλώσασθαι “IG” 4.955 (Epid., 2nd c.AD).}}
**II.6** in Mathematics texts, **contact** of surfaces, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 227a17, “Metaphorical” 1014b22, +others}}; **point of contact,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra “Phaenomena” p.16M., +others}}; **of intersection,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 988.9, compare 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in Aristotelis Topicorum libros octo commentaria - pars” 24.16.}}
**II.7** in _plural_, **stripes, strokes,** {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.7.14, +others}}
**II.8** **infection,** especially of **leprosy,**{{LXX|LXX.Lev.13.6, +others}}: generally, **plague,** {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aquila LXX.Gen.12.17, 2nd c.AD: Aquila LXX, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Symmachus LXX.Exo.11.1.}}
**III** **junction, point of contact** in the body, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 326b12, 327a12}}; **ligament, Ep. Eph.**{{NT|4.16, NT.Col.2.19.}}

861:
** ἀφθαρ-σία**, ἡ,
**incorruption, immortality,** {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.28U., 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3.9, +others, LXX.Wis.2.23, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.37, +others, NT.Rom.2.7, 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis de Caelo commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 298.16, etc.}}
**II** **integrity, sincerity,** {{NT|NT.Eph.6.24.}}

862:
** ἄφθαρ-τος**, ον,
**uncorrupted, undecaying,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 67.37.}}
**II** **incorruptible,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.29U., +others, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3 “fragment” 88b, +others, 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 63, +others}}
**II.2** **eternal,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Anthologia Graeca” 0.85b18, “Cael.” 270a21}}; **immortal,** πνεῦμα{{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.Wis.12.1; τὸ πᾶν 1st c.BC(?): Ocellus Lucanus Philosophus 1.10, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.6; ψυχαί 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.62; θεός NT.Rom.1.23; γένος 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.689; οἱ νεκροὶ ἐγερθήσονται ἄ. NT.1Cor.15.52}}; of poems, Μοῦσαι{{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 226.5 (from Teos).}}_adverb_ -τως, τιμᾶν{{Refs|[prev. work]919.10 (from Lycia).}}

863:
** ἀφίημι**,
_2nd pers.singular_ ἀφίης{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 50d, etc., _3rd.pers._ _sing._ ἀφίησι, also ἀφίει}} _Ionic dialect_ ἀπίει{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.96}}, _1st pers.plural_ ἀφίεμεν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1426; _imperative_ ἀφίει [prev. author] “V.” 428:}}_imperfect_ ἀφίειν, with double augment ἠφίειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 293a}}; _3rd.pers.singular_ ἀφίει{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.25, “IG” 22.777.15, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.20}}, _Ionic dialect_ ἀπίει{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.157, ἠφίει 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 222b, ἤφιε NT.Mark.11.16}}; _2nd pers.plural_ ἀφίετε{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.188}}; _3rd.pers.plural_ ἀφίεσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 821, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.76, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.79, etc., ἠφίεσαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.6.11, ἠφίουν 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.40 (uncertain)}}: _future_ ἀφήσω{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.263, etc.}}, _Ionic dialect_ ἀπ-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.193}}: _perfect_ ἀφεῖκα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.13, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.26:}}_aorist_ I ἀφῆκα, _Ionic dialect_ἀπ-, _Epic dialect_ ἀφέηκα, used in _indicative_ only, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.841, etc.:}}_aorist 2_ _indicative_ only in _dual_ and _plural_, ἀφέτην, ἀφεῖμεν, ἀφεῖτε or ἄφετε, ἀφεῖσαν or ἄφεσα; _imperative_ ἄφες, _subjunctive_ ἀφῶ, _optative_ αφείην (_2nd pers.plural_ ἀφεῖτε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.139)}}_infinitive_ ἀφεῖναι, _participle_ ἀφείς:—_middle_, ἀφίεμαι, _Ionic dialect_ ἀπίεμαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.101, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.60, etc.}}: _imperfect_ 3rd.pers._singular_ ἀφίετο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.240, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.47}}: _future_ ἀφήσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1629}}: _aorist 2_ ἀφείμην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 7.11}}; _imperative_ ἀφοῦ, ἄφεσθε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1521, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 509}}; _infinitive_ **-έσθαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.83}}, _participle_ **-έμενος**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 354b}}; Arc._infinitive_ ἀφεῶσθαι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 306.19(Tegea, 4th c.BC)}}:—_passive_, _perfect_ ἀφεῖμαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1165, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 635a}}; _infinitive_ ἀφεῖσθαι{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 577.77 (Milet., 3rd-2nd c.BC)}}: _pluperfect_ _2nd pers.singular_ ἀφεῖσο{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 572}}: rarer _perfect_ _3rd.pers.plural_ ἀφέωνται{{NT|NT.John.20.23, _imperative_ ἀφεώσθω “IG” 5(2).6.14}}: _aorist_ ἀφείθην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1377, ἀφέθην Batrachomyomachia 87}}, _Ionic dialect_ ἀπείθην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.112}}; later _Aeolic dialect_ _infinitive_ ἀφέθην{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Milet.” 3 No.152.34 (2nd c.BC)}}: _future_ ἀφεθήσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 472a, etc. [ῐ mostly in _Epic dialect_ (except in augment tenses)}}: ῑ always in _Attic dialect_ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus also has ἀφῑετε, for the sake of meter, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.126]}}:—**send forth, discharge,** of missiles, ἔγχος, δίσκον ἀφῆκεν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.372, 23.432; ἀφῆκ᾽ ἀργῆτα κεραυνόν 8.133; ἀπῆκε βέλος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.18, etc.: hence in various senses, ἀ. ἑαυτὸν ἐπί τι}} **throw oneself** upon, **give oneself up** to it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 373d; ἀ. αὑτὸν εἰς τὴν πολιτείαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 13}}; ἀ. γλῶσσαν **let loose** one's tongue, **make** utterance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.15, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 991}}; ἀ. φθογγήν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 418; ἔπος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 731; φωνάς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.218; γόους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 59 (see. below 11.2); ἀρὰς ἀφῆκας παιδί [prev. author] “Hipp.” 1324}}; ἀ. θυμὸν ἔς τινας **give vent to**.. (see.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|below 11.2), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1088}}; ὀργὴν εἴς τινα **vent** upon.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.58}}; ἀ. δάκρυα **shed** tears, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.153}}; ἀ. παντοδαπὰ χρώματα **change** colour in all ways, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 222b}}; frequently of liquids, etc., **emit,** ἀ. τὸ ὑγρόν, τὸν θολόν, τὸ σπέρμα, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 487a18, 524a12, 489a9; ἀ. τὸ ᾠόν, τὸ κύημα, [prev. work] 568b30, a22}}; of plants, ἄνθος ἀφιεῖσαι **putting forth,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.126, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 7.7.3}}; of a spider, ἀ. ἀράχνιον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 555b5; ἱδρῶτα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 26}}; **put forth, produce,** καρπόν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.4.5; φύλλον [prev. work] 6.5.1 (but ἀ. σπέρμα}} **leave** issue, {{NT|NT.Mark.12.22)}}:—_passive_, **to be emitted,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.77 (tmesis)}}; of troops, **to be let go, launched** against the enemy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.112.}}
**2** **let fall** from one's grasp, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.221}}; opposed to κατέχω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.508d; πόντιον ἀ. τινά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 797.}}
**3** **give up** or **hand over to,** τὴν Ἰωνίην τοῖσι βαρβάροισι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.106; ἐχθροῖς αἶαν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 306; ἀ. τινὰ δημόσια εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13}}:—_passive_, ἡ Ἀττικὴ ἀπεῖτο ἤδη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.49.}}
**II** **send away,**
**II.1** of persons, κακῶς ἀφίει{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.25; αὐτὸν δὲ κλαίοντα.. ἀφήσω 2.263.}}
**II.1.b** **let go, loose, set free,** ζωόν τινα ἀ.{{Refs|20.464}}; **let loose,** βοῦς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.69; περιστεράς 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 62.3; ἀ. Αἴγιναν αὐτόνομον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.139}}; ἀ. ἐλεύθερον, ἀζήμιον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 591a, “Lg.” 765c; τινὰς ἀφορολογήτους 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.46.5; ἀφέντ᾽ ἐᾶν τινα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 754, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 463}}; ἐς οἴκους, ἐκ γῆς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 320, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 739}}: with _accusative_ _person_ et _genitive_ _of things_, **release from** a thing, ἀποικίης{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.157}}: in legal sense, **acquit of** a charge or engagement, φόνου τινα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.59 (_absolutely_, ἐὰν αἰδέσηται καὶ ἀφῇ [prev. passage])}}; συναλλαγμάτων{{Refs|[prev. author] 33.12}}: with _accusative_ only, **acquit,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.2, etc. (see. below 2 c)}}:—_passive_, κινδύνου ἀφιέμενοι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.106}}; τοὺς γέροντας τοὺς ἀφειμένους **released** from duty, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1275a15; ἐγκλημάτων ἀφεῖσο 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 572.}}
**II.1.c** **let go, dissolve, disband,** of an army or fleet, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.77, etc.}}; **dismiss,** δικαστήρια (opposed to λύειν ἐκκλησίαν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 595.}}
**II.1.d** **put away, divorce,** γυναῖκα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.39}}; ἀ. γάμους **break off** a marriage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 973}}; ἀ. τὸν υἱόν **disown** him, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1163b22}} (but with _metaphorically_ from **releasing** a debtor).
**II.1.e** **dedicate,** τὰ νεογνὰ τῷ θεῷ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.14; ἱερὸν.. ἄβατον ἀφεῖτο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 116c.}}
**II.2** of things, **get rid of,** ἀφέτην πολυκαγκέα δίψαν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.642}}; ἀφίει μένος [ἔγχεος] **slackened** its force, {{Refs|13.444}}; ἀ. ὀργήν **put away** wrath (see.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|above 1.1), 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 317; ὀργήν τινι 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 1.10.6}}; γόους{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(see. above 1.1) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1022; νόσημα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.39}}; ἀ. πνεῦμα, ψυχήν, **give up** the ghost, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 571, “Or.” 1171}}: in Prose, **give up, leave off,** μόχθον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.206}}; ξυμμαχίαν, σπονδάς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.78, 115, etc.}}:—_middle_, ἀ. τὸ προλέγειν{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.1.}}
**II.2.b** ἀ. πλοῖον ἐς.. **loose** ship for a place, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.42.}}
**II.2.c** in legal sense (see. supr. Ib), with _dative_ _person_ et _accusative_ _of things_, ἀ. τινὶ αἰτίην **remit** him a charge, {{NT+4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.30; τὰς ἁμαρτάδας [prev. author] 8.140.β᾽,cf. NT.Matt.6.12, +others; τὰς δίκας.. ἀφίεσαν τοῖς ἐπιτρόποις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.79; ἀ. τινὶ εἰς ἐλευθερίαν χιλίας δραχμάς [prev. author] 59.30, compare “IG” 22.43 A 27}}; ἀ. πληγάς τινι **excuse** him a flogging, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1426}}; ἀ. ὅρκον Jusj. in Legal cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.98; φόρον 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.24.8 (_passive_)}}; δάνειόν τινι{{NT|NT.Matt.18.27.}}
**III** **leave alone, pass by,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.95, etc.}}; **neglect,** τὰ θεῖα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1537; τὸν καιρόν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.8; λέκτρων εὐνάς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 544: followed by a predicate, ἀφύλακτον ἀ. τὴν ἑωυτῶν}} **leave** unguarded, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.70}}; ἄτιμον, ἔρημον ἀ. τινά, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1279, “Ant.” 887; ἀ. τινὰς ὀρφανούς NT.John.14.18; ἀ. τι ἀόριστον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1265a39}}; **leave,** περὶ κινήσεως, ὅθεν ὑπάρχει, τοῖς ἄλλοις ἀφεῖσαν{{Refs|[prev. author] “Metaphorical” 985b20, compare 987b14}}:—_passive_, especially in _perfect_ _imperative_, ἀφείσθω ἐπὶ τοῦ παρόντος **missum fiat,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1166a34, compare “Pol.” 1286a5, 1289b12.}}
**III.2** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, ἀ. τὸ πλοῖον φέρεσθαι **let** the boat be carried away, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.194; μὴ ἀφεῖναί με ἐπὶ ξένης ἀδιαφορηθῆναι “PLond.” 2.144.14(1st c.AD).}}
**IV** with _accusative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, **suffer, permit** one to do a thing, ἀ. τινὰ ἀποπλέειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.25, compare 6.62, +others, etc.: with}}_infinitive_ understood, ἡνίκα προῖκ᾽ ἀφιᾶσιν (i.e. θεᾶσθαι) οἱ θεατρῶναι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 30.6}}: with _subjunctive_, ἄφες ἐκβάλω{{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Matt.7.4, compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.9.15; ἄφες ἐγὼ θρηνήσω “POxy.” 413.184 (1st c.AD)}}; ἄφες ἵνα.. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 4.13.19}}; οὐκ ἤφιεν ἵνα.. {{NT|NT.Mark.11.16}}:—_passive_, ἀφείθη σχολάζειν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 981b24.}}
**V** seemingly _intransitive_ (i.e. στρατόν, ναῦς, etc.), **break up, march, sail,** etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.193; ἀ. ἐς τὸ πέλαγος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.19; compare 11.2b.}}
**V.2** with _infinitive_, **give up** doing, ἀφεὶς σκοπεῖν τὰ δίκαια{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 94.}}
**B** _middle_, **send forth from oneself,** much like _active_; θορήν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.101.}}
**B.2** **loose** something **of one's own from,** δειρῆς δ᾽ οὔ πω.. ἀφίετο πήχεε λευκώ she **loosed** not **her** arms **from off** my neck, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.240.}}
**B.3** frequently in _Attic dialect_ with _genitive_ only, τέκνων ἀφοῦ **let go of** the children ! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1521; τοῦ κοινοῦ τῆς σωτηρίας ἀ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.60; λόγων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 458c, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.178; μὴ ἀφίεσο τοῦ Θεαιτήτου, ἀλλ᾽ ἐρώτα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 146b, etc.; ἀφεῖσθαι τοῦ δικαίου τούτου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.1; ἀφέμενος τῆς ἰαμβικῆς ἰδέας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1449b8.}}

864:
** ἀφικνέομαι**,
_Ionic dialect_ ἀπ- {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.28, +others}}: _imperfect_ ἀφικνεῖτο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.33}}: _future_ ἀφίξομαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.270, etc.}}, _Ionic dialect_ _2nd pers.singular_ ἀπίξεαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.29}}, _3rd.pers.singular_ ἀπίξεται{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 29.13}}: _perfect_ ἀφῖγμαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.297}}, _Attic dialect_ _2nd pers.singular_ ἀφῖξαι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 305}}, _3rd.pers.singular_ ἀφῖκται{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 794:}}_pluperfect_ ἀφῖκτο{{Refs|[prev. work] 1590}}, _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers.plural_ ἀπίκατο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.6}}: _aorist_ ἀφῑκόμην{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.395, etc.}}; _infinitive_ ἀφικέσθα; _Doric dialect_ _imperative_ ἀφίκευσο{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.42}}: _aorist 1_ _participle_ ἀφιξάμενος{{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 981.9 (from Philae)}}:—**arrive at, come to, reach:** Constr., in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus, and Trag. mostly with _accusative_ loci, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.645, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.29, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 15, etc.; ὅνδε δόμονδε 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 38: in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus also with _accusative_ _person_, μνηστῆρας ἀ.}} **came up to** them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.332, compare 11.122, etc.}}; ὅτε μ᾽ ἄλγος ἀφίκετο **came to** me, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.395}}; similarly, τοῦτον νῦν ἀφίκεσθε **come up to** this throw ! {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.202}}; frequently also with Preps., ἀ. ἐς.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.431, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.255, etc.}}; less frequently ἐπί.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.281, 22.208}}; still more rarely κατά.., ποτί..,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|13.329, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.297; ἀ. πρὸς τέλος γόων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1622; ἐπὶ τῶν νήσων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.2; ἄχρι τοῦ μὴ πεινῆν ἀ. [prev. author] “Smp.” 4.37; θανάτου τοῦτ᾽ ἐγγυτάτω τοὔπος ἀφῖκται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 934; παρά τινος ἀ. [prev. author] “OT” 935, etc.}}: _absolutely_, **arrive,** ὁπποίης ἐπὶ νηὸς ἀφίκεο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.171; ὅταν ἀ. ὥρη 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 723; σῖτος ἀφικνούμενος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.31; ὁ ἀφικνούμενος}}**the stranger, newcomer,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.118.11:}} —Phrases:
**1** ἀ. ἐπὶ or εἰς πάντα, **to try** every means, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 265, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 284; ἀ. ἐς πᾶσαν βάσανον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.110; ἐς διάπειράν τινος ἀ. [prev. author] 2.28, 77; ἐπὶ τὸ τέμνειν μῦς ἀ. 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.230.}}
**2** **come into** a certain condition, ἀ. ἐς πᾶν κακοῦ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.118; ἐς ἀπορίην πολλήν [prev. author] 1.79}}; ἐς τοσοῦτο τύχης, ἐς τοῦτο δυστυχίας, **come into** such a..,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 124, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.86}}; ἐς ὀλίγον ἀ. νικηθῆναι **come** within little of being conquered, {{Refs|Id 4.129}}; εἰς τὸ ἴσον ἀ. τινί **attain** equality with.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.5; εἰς ὀργήν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 44}}; elliptically, ἐς ἄνδρ᾽ ἀφίκου **reachedst** man's estate, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 322.}}
**3** of intercourse with others, ἀ. τινὶ ἐς λόγους **hold** converse with one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.28}}; ἐς ἔχθεα, ἐς ἔριν ἀ. τινί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.82, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 319}}; διὰ μάχης, δι᾽ ἔχθρας ἀ. τινί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.169, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1164; διὰ λόγων ἐμαυτῇ [prev. author] “Med.” 872.}}
**3.b** less frequently with _dative_ _person_, ἀ. τινί **come at** his **call,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.67, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.24, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.85.}}
**4** εἰς τόξευμα ἀ. **come** within shot, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.23, etc.}}
**5** of things, ἐς ὀξὺ ἀ. uncertain reading for ἀπηγμένα, -μένας in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.28, 7.64; ὁ λόγος εἰς ταὐτὸν ἀ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1097a24, compare 1167a12, +others}}
**II** the sense of **return** is sometimes implied in the context, but is not inherent in the word, as {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.420, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.54, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 6, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 153a.}}

867:
** ἄφιξις**, εως,
_Ionic dialect_ ἄπιξις, ιος, ἡ: (ἀφικνέομαι):— **arrival,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.69, +others}}; τὴν ἀπὸ Κορίνθου ἄ. **arrival** from C., {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.92. ; ἐς Θήβας [prev. author] 9.17; ἄ. ἐπὶ Σαρπηδονίης ἄκρης ποιεῖσθαι [prev. author] 7.58}}; μέρος αὐτῆς ἀπίξιος παρὰ τὸν μάγον her turn **for going in** to him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.69, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 337e, Aristeas 173, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “contra Apionem” 1.18, etc.; ἄ. εἰς τοὺς πολεμίους ποιησάμενος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.8; ἄ. εἰς Κύπρον 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.53; ἡ ἐνθάδε ἄ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.145, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.26; ἡ οἴκαδε ἄ.}}**home again,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 1.2, 3.39.}}
**2** **departure,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 2.2.4, NT.Act.20.29.}}
**II** ={ἱκεσία}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 483.}}

868:
** ἀφίστημι**:
causal in _present_ and _imperfect_, in _future_ ἀποστήσω, and _aorist_ I ἀπέστησα, as also in _aorist_ I _middle_ (see. below):—**put away, remove, keep out of the way,** τὸ ἀσθενέστατον τοῦ στρατεύματος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.5.23; ἄχος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 416 (Lyric poetry)}}; of diseases, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.96, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.846; τὰ συγκείμενα ἀ. ἀπ᾽ ἀλλήλων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 282b; ἀ. τῆς ἐλάας τὸν φλοιόν 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.3.2; ἀ. τινὰ λόγου}}**hinder from..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 912}}; ἀ. τὰς τῶν πολεμίων ἐπιβουλάς **frustrate** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.93}}; ἀ. τὸν ἄρχοντα **depose** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.45:}} —_aorist_ _middle_, Ἀργείων δόρυ πυλῶν ἀπεστήσασθε **remoued it from your own** gates, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus ph.1087:—in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.23 ἀποστήσαντες, ={ἀποστάντες}}}], **having retired.**
**2** **cause to revolt,** of allies, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.19, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 238, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.81; τινὰ ἀπό τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.76, 154, etc.; τινά τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.22.}}
**3** in _geometrical_ constructions, **cut off,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Hypotyposis astronomicarum positionum” 6.7.}}
**II** **weigh out,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.20; ἀποστησάτωσαν τὰ χρυσία “IG” 7.303.19 (from Oropus)}}; **pay,** δραχμὰς ή ἀργυρίου{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 93.2 (2nd c.BC): also in}}_aorist_ I _middle_, μὴ.. ἀποστήσωνται Ἀχαιοὶ χρεῖος lest they **weigh out** (i. e. **pay in full**) the debt, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.745, compare “IG” 12.91.20, +others:}} —in strict sense of _middle_, ἀποστήσασθαι τὸν χαλκόν **to have** the brass **weighed out to one,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 49.52.— 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has it _transitive_ only in [prev. cited]}}
**III** _middle_, **give a final decision** (or **break up, dismiss** the assembly), ῥήτρα cited in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 6.}}
**B** _intransitive_, in _passive_, as also in _aorist 2_ ἀπέστην, _imperative_ ἀπόστηθι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 627, ἀπόστα 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 375:}}_perfect_ ἀφέστηκα in _present_ sense, syncopated. in _plural_ ἀφέστᾰμεν, -στᾰτε, -στᾶσι, as in _infinitive_ ἀφεστάναι, _participle_ ἀφεστώς, -ῶσα, -ός or -ώς: _pluperfect_ ἀφεστήκειν, _Attic dialect_ **-κη**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 208e}}: _future_ _middle_ ἀποστήσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1054, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.64, etc. (while}}_aorist_ I _middle_ is causal (see. above)): for _future_ ἀφεστήξω see entry .:—**stand away** or **aloof from, keep far from,** ὅσσον δὲ τροχοῦ ἵππος ἀφίσταται{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.517; οὐ μέν κ᾽ ἄλλη γ᾽ ὧδε γυνὴ.. ἀνδρὸς ἀφεσταίη 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.101; ἀποστᾶσ᾽ ἐκποδών 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1023; ἐς ἄλλο σχῆμ᾽ ἀ. βίου [prev. author] “Med.” 1039; ἀποσταθῶμεν πράγματος τελουμένου 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 872}}; ὡς γραφεὺς (or βραβεὺς) ἀποσταθείς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 807; μακρὰν τόποις καὶ χρόνοις ἀ. 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.22: hence in various relations, ἀφεστάναι φρενῶν}} **lose** one's wits, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 865; φύσεος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1457 (Lyric poetry)}}; οὐδενὸς ἀποστήσονται ὅσα ἂν δίκαια λέγητε **depart** **from, object to** right proposals, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.118; ἀ. φόνου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1544}}; ἀ. ἀρχῆς **to be deposed from** office, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 928d}}; simply, **resign**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 527.105 (Crete, 3rd c.BC)}}; ἀ. τῶν πραγμάτων, τῆς πολιτείας, etc., **withdraw from** business, politics, **have done with** it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.1, 18.308, etc.}}; ἔργων ἢ πόνων ἢ κινδύνων **shun** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.83, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.5.19, etc.}}; ὧν εἷλεν ἀποστάς **giving up all claim to** what he had won (at law), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.181; τῶν αὑτῆς [prev. author] 19.147, compare 35.4; ἀφίστασθαι τῶν τοῦ ἀδελφοῦ [prev. work]44; οὐδενὸς τῶν ἀνηκόντων τῇ πόλει “Inscription Magn.” 53.65}}; τὴν πολιτείαν.. τὴν ἀφεστηκυῖαν τοῦ μέσου πλεῖον further **removed from..,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1296b8; ἀποστὰς τῶν πατρῴων 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 12.3}}; ἀ. ἐκ Σικελίας **withdraw** from the island, **give up** the expedition, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.28}}; **retire,** ἐς Ἰθώμην{{Refs|[prev. author] 1.101}}: rarely c._accusative_, **avoid, shrink from,** τὸν ἥλιον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 3.3; τὸν πόλεμον [prev. author] “An.” 2.5.7; τινάς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1006; πυγμήν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “de gymnastica” 20 (probably conjecture).}}
**B.2** in Prose, ἀ. ἀπό τινος **revolt** from.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.95, 130, etc.; τινός [prev. author] 2.113; οὐκ ἀποστήσομαι ἀπὸ τοῦ δήμου Ἀθηναίων “IG” 12.39.21; but Ἀθηναίων τοῦ πλήθους [prev. work]10.22; also ἀ. εἴς or πρός τινας, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.30, 162, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.7; ἐς δημοκρατίαν ἀ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.90}}: _absolutely_, **revolt,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.102, etc.}}; ὑπό τινος at his instigation, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.35 (ἀπό codices).}}
**B.3** ἀ. τινί **make way for** another, **give way to** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1054, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.37.}}
**B.4** with _genitive_, **shrink from,** τῶν κινδύνων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.29}}: also with _infinitive_, **shrink from** doing, ἀπέστην τοῦτ᾽ ἐρωτῆσαι σαφῶς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 536.}}
**B.5** _absolutely_, **stand aloof, recoil** from fear, horror, etc., τίπτε καταπτώσσοντες ἀφέστατ;{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.340; πολλὸν ἀφεσταότες 17.375; παλίνορσος ἀπέστη 3.33, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.52, “P.” 4.145; ἐγὼ οὐδέν τι μᾶλλον ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 169b, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.45, etc.}}; **desist,** μὴ νῦν -ώμεθα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 960e; δοῦλος ἀφεστώς}}**a runaway,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 23.7.}}
**B.6** in Medicine texts, ἀφίσταται, ={ἀπόστασις γίγνεται, εἰς ἄρθρα} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 4.74}}; ἀ. ὀστέον **exfoliates,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 6.45}}; also ἀ. ἀπὸ τῶν ὀστῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 84a; τὸ δέρμα ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.5}}; also, **project, stand out,** ὦτα ἀφεστηκότα{{Refs 1st c.BC+|“PLond.” 3.1209.12 (1st c.BC).}}
**B.6.b** **to be separated** by the formation of an abscess, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.116, +others}} (also in _active_, τὸ πύον ἀφίστησι 7.715).

870:
Included with: ** ἄφοβος**, ον,
**without fear,** and so:
**1** **fearless, intrepid,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).40, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1325, etc.}}; πρὸς ἐρημίαν, περὶ τοῦ μέλλοντος, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 16, “Galb.” 23}}: with _genitive_, **having no fear of,** τῶν ἀρχόντων{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 2.52}}; τὸ ἄφοβον, ={ἀφοβία}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 197b}}. _adverb_ **-βως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 7.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 682c, “PTeb.” 24.74(2nd c.BC).}}
**2** **causing no fear, free from fear,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Pr.” 902; λόγος οὐκ ἄ. εἰπεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 797a.}}
**3** ἄ. θῆρες, in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 366}}, is an oxymoron, beasts **which fear not men** or **which no one fears, tame** beasts, **cattle.**

871:
** ἀφομοι-όω**,
**make like,** τινί τι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 9.9; τοῖς γράμμασι τὰ ἔργα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 427c; μαινομένοις.. ἀ. αὑτούς [prev. author] “R.” 396a; [τῶν θεῶν] τὰ εἴδη ἑαυτοῖς ἀ. οἱ ἄνθρωποι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1252b27}}:—also in _middle_ or _passive_, **become** or **be made like,** τινί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 396b, +others; πρός τι [prev. author] “Sph.” 240a, etc.}}
**II** **compare,** τινί τι{{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 517b, 564b.}}
**III** with _accusative_ _of things_, **portray,** of painters, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.10.2}}: _absolutely_, **make a copy,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 424d.}}

872:
** ἀφορ-άω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **ἀπορ-έω**, _future_ ἀπόψομαι: _aorist_ ἀπεῖδον:— **look away from** all others at one, **have in view,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.37}}; **look at,** τι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 17}}; of a distant object, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 2.20; πρός τι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.71; τι πρός τι}}**compare,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 585a}}; ἀ. ὅθεν.. **look to the point** whence.., {{Refs|[prev. work] 584d}}; εἴς τι or τινα, {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 7, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philops.” 30; εἰς τὸν θεόν 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.19.29; εἰς τὸν Ἰησοῦν NT.Heb.12.2}}: hence, **obey,**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|Nic.Dam. p.22 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; ἐπί τινα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 52; πρός τινα [prev. author] “Cat.Ma.” 19, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.24.16}}:—in _middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 281.}}
**b** of Places, **look towards,** εἰς βόρειον ὠκεανόν{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Praefatio” 3,compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Im.Prooem.”}}
**2** **to view from a place,** ἀπὸ δενδρέου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.22; πόρρωθεν ἀπιδών 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 21.6.}}
**II** rarely, **look away, have the back turned,** probably in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.16.6; ἀφορῶντας παίειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.36.}}

873:
** ἀφορ-ίζω**,
**mark off by boundaries,** ἐξελόντας [τὸ ὄρος] τῷ θεῷ καὶ ἀφορίσαντας{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 16}}; οὐσία ἀφωρισμένη property **marked out by boundary-pillars,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 49.61}}:—_middle_, **mark off for oneself, appropriate to oneself,** χώραν ὅτι πλείστην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.120: _metaphorically_, ἀ. τιμάς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 31}}: —_passive_, ἡ ὑπό τινος ἀφορισθεῖσα χώρα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.36.}}
**b** **border on,** τοὺς ὅρους -ίζοντας τὸν Ἀσωπόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 110e.}}
**2** **determine, define,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sph.” 240c}}:—also _middle_, περὶ φύσεως ἀφοριζόμενοι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Epicrates Comicus 11.13:}} — _passive_, χρόνος ἀφωρισμένος a **determinate** time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 785b}}; ὅροι -ισμένοι **well-defined,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Criti.” 110d}}; ἀφωρισμένα **definite cases,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1354b8}}: _participle_ ἀφορίσας **definitely,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.29.}}
**3** **separate, distinguish,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Sophista “Oxy.” 1364.290 (_passive_)}}; **exclude,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 501d, +others, ἀ. χωρίς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1331a27; ἀ. τί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 298d; ἱππέων ἕδρας ἀπὸ τῶν ἄλλων 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 36.42}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 644a, “Chrm.” 173e; τι ἀπό τινος [prev. author] “Sph.” 227c}}:—_passive_, ἀφορίζεσθαι ἀπό τινος{{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 205c: _absolutely_, ἀγνοίας ἀφωρισμένον εἶδος}} **distinct** species, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Sph.” 229c; ἐπιστήμη ἀφωρισμένη 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1354a3.}}
**4** **bring to an end, finish,** βίβλους{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.71.10}}:— _middle_, λόγον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.58.}}
**5** **grant as a special gift,** τᾷ κάλλος ἀφώρισε Κύπρις{{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 244.3 (from Cyzicus).}}
**II** with _accusative_ _person_,
**II.1** **banish,** καί μ᾽ ἀπὸ γᾶς ὥρισε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 940 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.2** **separate,** {{NT|NT.Acts.19.9, etc.}}:—_passive_, ἱερέων γένος ἀπὸ τῶν ἄλλων-ισμένον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 24a}}; ἐκ τινῶν ἀφωρισμένων from **a definite class** of persons, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1292b4}}; ἀφωρισμένος τέχνην **having a definite** art **assigned one,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 231e.}}
**II.2.b** **set apart** for rejection, **cast out, excommunicate,** {{NT|NT.Luke.6.22.}}
**II.2.c** **set apart for** some office, **appoint, ordain,** {{NT|NT.Acts.13.2, NT.Rom.1.1 (_passive_), NT.Gal.1.15.}}
**II.2.d** _passive_, ἀρχὴ ἀφωρισμένη πρὸς τὰς θυσίας{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1322b26}}; of a treatise, **to be devoted to,** with _dative_, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteora commentaria - pars” 9.15.}}

874:
** ἀφορμ-ή**, ἡ,
**starting-point,** especially in war, **base of operations,** ἀναχώρησίς τε καὶ ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.90, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.41.6, etc.}}; **place of safety,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 342.}}
**2** generally, **starting-point, origin, occasion** or **pretext,** ἀφορμαὶ λόγων{{NT+5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hec.” 1239, “Ph.” 199; ἀφορμὴν παρέχειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.156; δεδωκέναι [prev. author] 21.98, cf. NT.2Cor.5.12; λαβεῖν ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.61, NT.Rom.7.8; εὑρεῖν “BGU” 615.6(2nd c.AD), 923.22 (1st-2nd c.AD)}}; ἵνα ἀ. γένοιτο τιμῆς{{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Inscription Prien.” 105.16 (1st c.BC)}}; ἀ. καὶ πρόφασις{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.52.3}}; **occasion, origin** of an illness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 2.1.11, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.29}}; εἰδέ τις οἴεται μικρὰν ἀ. εἶναι σιτηρέσιον τοῖς στρατευομένοις ὑπάρχειν a small **inducement,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.29; τὸ γὰρ εὖ πράττειν παρὰ τὴν ἀξίαν ἀ. τοῦ κακὼς φρονεῖν [prev. author] 1.23}}; **instigation, incitement,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 237 vii 21 (2nd c.AD).}}
**3** **means with which one begins** a thing, **resources,** ἀ. τοῦ βίου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.24; εἰς τὸν βίον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.12.4; τίνας εἶχεν ἀφορμὰς ἡ πόλι; 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.233; ἀφελεῖν τὴν ἀ. δι᾽ ἣν ὑβρίζει [prev. author] 21.98}}; πίστις ἀ. μεγίστη πρὸς χρηματισμόν good faith is the best **asset** for business, {{Refs|[prev. author] 36.44, compare 11.16}}; ἀ. ἐπί.. {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.33}}; especially **means of war,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.109; ἀ. εἰς ξένους χιλίους}}**means for levying** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1000 mercenaries, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.8.33}}; ἀ. ἔργων **means for undertaking**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Mem.” 2.7.11, compare 3.5.11; πρὸς ἀφορμὴν ἐμπορίας ἢ γεωργίας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1320a39; πάντων ἀ. τῶν καλῶν 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 110.}}
**4** **capital** of a banker, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 1.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.12, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 26, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 14.30, 36.11}}; ἀφορμῆς δίκη suit for **restitution of capital,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Arg. Demosthenes Orator 36.}}
**5** Rhet., **food for argument, material, subject,** ὑποθέσεις καὶ ἀφορμαὶ λόγων{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Rh.Pr.” 18, cf. Men.Rh. p.334S., Aps. p.264H.}}
**6** **aptitude, inclination,** εἰς φιλανθρωπίαν{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.53 W. (_plural_).}}
**II** Stoic term for **repulsion** (opposed to ὁρμή), {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.42, compare 40, 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Epictetum commentaria” p.22D.}}
**III** **release** of water from sluice, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PAmh.” 2.143.17 (4th c.AD).}}

876:
** ἀφρός**, ὁ,
**foam,** of the sea, ῥόος Ὠκεανοῖο ἀφρῷ μορύρων{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.403}}, etc.; of a river, {{Refs|5.599.}}
**2** of persons and animals, **foam, slaver, froth,** περί τ᾽ ἀ. ὀδόντας γίγνεται{{NT+5th c.BC+|20.168; ἀ. περὶ στόμα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 2.43, cf. NT.Luke.9.39; μέλαν᾽ ἀπ᾽ ἀνθρώπων ἀφρόν}}**frothy blood,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 183, compare “fragment” 372; θρομβώδεις ἀφροί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 702}}; βακχίου παλαιγενοῦς ἀφρῷ, of wine, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 237; κύλικα.. ἀφρῷ ζέουσαν Theophilus Comicus 2.}}
**II** ἀφρὸς νίτρου, ={ἀφρόνιτρον}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.75}}; ἀ. alone, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Coloribus” 794a20.}}
**II.2** ἀ. αἵματος, ={σπέρμα},{{Refs 4th c.BC+|Diog.Apoll.A 24 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**III** a kind of ἀφύη, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 569a29, b28, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 7.325b; Ionic, _accusative_ to 4th c.BC: Archestratus Epicus “fragment” 9.2, but compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus see {ἀφύων τιμή}}}]. (**ṃbhrós,** cf. Sanskrit _abhrám_ 'cloud', Latin _imber._)

878:
** ἄφρων**, ον,
_genitive_ ονος, (φρήν) **senseless,** of statues, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.4}}:— and so, **crazed, frantic,** ἄφρονα κούρην{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.875, compare 761, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 377 (Lyric poetry)}}; **silly, foolish,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.220, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” [210], 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 941, etc.; φρένας ἄ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.104}}; τὸ ἄ., ={ἀφροσύνη}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.105, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.55}}; τῶ φρονίμῳ τῆς γνώμης παύοντες τὸ ἄ. [τῆς ῥώμης] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “fragment” 6; ἐξ ἄφρονος σώφρων γεγένηται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.17}}: _comparative_ **-έστερος**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 392c}}: _superlative_ **-έστατος**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.5}}. _adverb_ **-νως** **senselessly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 766, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.19}}; opposed to νοῦν ἐχόντως, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.7}}: _comparative_ **-εστέρως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 193c}} **-έστερον** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.224d.}}
**2** ἄφρων, ={κώνειον}, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.78.}}

879:
** ἀφυπν-όω**,
**awake from sleep,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 9.517 (Antip. Thess.), 1st-2nd c.AD: Antonius Diogenes Scriptor Eroticus 9.}}
**II** **fall asleep,** {{NT+7th c.AD+|NT.Luke.8.23, 7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus 1.98}}:—_middle_, [{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 9.12 (variant{ὑφυπν-}).}}]

880:
** ἄφων-ος**, ον,
(φωνή) **voiceless, dumb,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 669, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.85, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.191; ῥήτωρ 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 196.14; κακὸν ἄ. “Comica Adespota” 8 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; stronger than ἄναυδος (which see), {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 3.17. ; εἴδωλα NT.1Cor.12.2}}; **unable to speak,** of a child, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 118: with _genitive_, ἄ. τῆσδε τῆς ἀρᾶς}} **unable to utter** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 865}}. _adverb_ **-νως**{{Refs|[prev. work] 131 (Lyric poetry)}}: _neuter_ _plural_ as _adverb_, ἄφωνα σημανοῦσιν.. ὡς.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 819.}}
**2** **with a poor voice,** τραγῳδός{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 631.21.}}
**3** **intestate,** {{Refs|Tab.Heracl. 1.151.}}
**4** ἄφωνα (i.e. γράμματα, στοιχεῖα) **consonants,** ἄ. καὶ φωνοῦντα (perhaps ἄ. φωνήεντα) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 578.2; τοῖς ἄλλοις φωνήεσί τε καὶ ἄ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 393e; τὸ σῖγμα τῶν ἀ. ἐστί [prev. author] “Tht.” 203b}}: but especially of **mutes,** τὰ ἄφθογγα καὶ ἄ.{{Refs|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 18c, compare “Cra.” 424c}}; opposed to ἡμίφωνα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1456b28, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 2.16, “Herc.” 994.28, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 14, 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 631.20, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.102.}}

884:
** ἀχᾰριστ-ος**, ον,
(χᾰρίζομαι) **ungracious, unpleasant,** οὐκ ἀχάριστα μεθ᾽ ἡμῖν ταῦτ᾽ ἀγορεύεις{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.236: irregular _comparative_, δόρπου ἀχαρίστερον (for -ιστότερον) 20.392}}; **without grace** or **charm,** οὐκ ἀχάριστα λέγειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.13; φωνή 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 75}}; -ότερον ἐπιμέλημα a **more thankless** business, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 7.37; ἀ. ἐξέτασις 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Epistula ad Pompeium” 1.}}
**II** of persons, **ungracious, unfavourable,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 841 (-τως Bgk.), 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.60 W.}}
**II.2** **ungrateful, thankless,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.90, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.7, 4th c.BC: Crates Thebanus Poeta Philosophus 19, etc.; δῆμος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.91; προδότας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 880 (Lyric poetry), compare “Med.” 659 (Lyric poetry); ἀ. πρὸς τοὺς γονέας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.14; τινί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 138 (Lyric poetry)}}; σπείρων εἰς ἀχάριστα sowingin **thankless soil,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.2012 (Sulp. 1st c.BC(?): Maximus Astrologus)}}. _adverb_ -τως, ἀποπέμψασθαι εὐεργέτας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.23, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.6.}}
**II.3** _passive_, **unrequited,** ἀ. εἶναι τὰ ἀνηλωμένα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 21.12.}}_adverb_ οὐκ ἂν ἀχαρίστως μοι ἔχοι πρός τινος **thanks** would not be **refused** me by.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.18.}}
**II.4** _adverb_ -τως **with a bad grace, with an ill will,** ἀ. ἕπεσθαι follow **sulkily,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 7.4.14; τὰς χάριτας ἀ. χαρίζεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.31.}}
**II.5** ἀχάριστον, τό, **antidote,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PGrenf. 1.52.1,12 (3rd c.AD), Marcell. Empir.20: also ἀχάριστος, ἡ, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor Trall. “Febr.” 7.}}
**II.5.b** name of an **eyesalve,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Celsus Medicus 6.6.7, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.749.}}

887:
** ἀχλύς**, ύος, ἡ,
(_accusative_ ἀχλύα{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 341)}} **mist,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.357; elsewhere in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} of **a mist over the eyes,** as of one dying, κατὰ δ᾽ ὀφθαλμῶν κέχυτ᾽ ἀ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.696}}; as result of ulceration, ἀχλύες{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.20, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 7.6.2, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.78 (_plural_), 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 7.27}}; or in emotion, Ἔρως πολλὴν κατ᾽ ἀχλὺν ὀμμάτων ἔχευεν{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 103}}; of drunkenness, πρὸς ὄμμ᾽ ἀ. ἀμβλωπὸς ἐφίζει{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 6.11 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; of one whom a god deprives of the power of seeing and knowing others, κατ᾽ ὀφθαλμῶν χέεν ἀχλύν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.321; ἀπ᾽ ὀφθαλμῶν σκέδασ᾽ ἀχλύν [prev. work] 341, compare 5.127, 15.668}}:— personified as **Sorrow,** πὰρ δ᾽ Ἀχλὺς εἱστήκει ἐπισμυγερή τε καὶ αἰνή, χλωρή, ἀϋσταλέη{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 264.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, δνοφεράν τιν᾽ ἀχλὺν.. αὐδᾶται{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 379 (Lyric poetry), compare “Pers.” 668 (Lyric poetry); ἀχλὺν ἀπὸ τῆς ψυχῆς ἀφελεῖν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 38.19; διάνοια ἀχλύος γέμουσα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.42c.}}
**3** ἀ. ὑγρή liquid emitted by cuttlefish, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 3.158.}}—Mostly _poetry_, but used by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus (see. above) and 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 367b17, 373b12 (_plural_)}}: also in later Prose, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 34.11.15, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 6.2.8, and see above 2}}. [ῡ in _nominative_ and _accusative_ _singular_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus: ῠ in later poets.]}}

888:
** ἀχρεῖος**
(_Attic dialect_ ἄχρειος _accusative_ to Hdn.Gr. 1.136), ον, also α, ον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“IG” 7.303.10 (from Oropus), 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.5, Polemo Physiognomonicus 69}}: _Ionic dialect_ ἀχρήϊος:— **useless, unprofitable,** ἀχρήϊος ἀνήρ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 297}}; ἐπέων νομός{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 403; δέμας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 365, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐντὸς παθῶν” 39; οἰκητήρ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 627}}; opposed to εὐγενής, {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 667}}; ἐρινὸς ἀ. ὢν ἐς βρῶσιν{{NT+5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 181; ἀ. κοὐ σοφός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 299; δοῦλος NT.Matt.25.30; οὐκ ἀπράγμονα ἀλλ᾽ ἀχρεῖον νομίζομεν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.40; λόγον ἀχρεῖον ἀπέρριψαν 2nd c.AD(?): Antoninus Liberalis Mythographus 11.3}}: _comparative_ -ότερος, ὄρνιθες{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.195}}: with _infinitive_, **unfit** to do, ἀ. πράττειν τι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 371c: with _dative_, ἀ. τοῖς σώμασι 2nd c.BC: Agatharchides Geographus “Fragmenta Historica - FHG” 3, compare 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 26.326a.}}
**2** especially **unfit for war,** ἀ. ὅμιλος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.81}}; τὸ ἀ. τοῦ στρατοῦ **the unserviceable part** of an army, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.191; οἱ ἀχρειότατοι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.93, 2.6, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.18; τὸ ἀ. τῆς ἡλικίας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.44}}. _adverb_ -είως, ἔχειν πρὸς ναυμαχίαν{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 5.84.}}
**II** _neuter_ ἀχρεῖον, as _adverb_, twice in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἀχρεῖον ἰδών}} giving a **helpless** look, looking **foolish,** of Thersites after being beaten, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.269}}; ἀχρεῖον δ᾽ ἐγέλασσε laughed **without cause,** made a **forced** laugh, of Penelope trying to disguise her feelings, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.163}}; ἀχρεῖον κλάζειν bark **without need** or **cause,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.72.}}
**III** _adverb_ ἀχρείως γέλασόν με (compare ἀχρειόγελως) {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“APl.” 4.86, compare 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 33.367b}}: _neuter_ _plural_ as _adverb_ ἀχρεῖ᾽ ἀσπαίροντος **helplessly,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euphorio Epicus 44.}}

890:
** ἄχρησ-τος**, ον,
**useless, unprofitable,** μετάνοια{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 70; νέες 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.166; ἄ. ὁ ὀφθαλμὸς γίνεται 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.19; οὐκ ἄ. ἥδ᾽ ἡ ἄνοια 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.16}}; χρεομένῳ ἄχρηστα **useless** if you try to use them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 14; πεσεῖν ἄ. θέσφατον}}**without effect,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 121; ἄ. ἐς πόλεμον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.142, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 53; πρός τι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 560b14: with _genitive_ _of things_, ἄ. τῶν ἔργων [prev. author] “Oec.” 1345a35}}; ἄ. τινι **useless** to a person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 8.4 (_superlative_)}}; τῇ πόλει{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 4; τὸ διηπορηκέναι οὐκ ἄ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 8b24.}}
**2** ={ἀχρεῖος} (which it almost superseded in the Oratt. and later Greek), **useless, do-nothing,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.135 (_comparative_), etc.}}; ἄ. πολῖται{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.37}}; σοφισταί probable reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 33.3}}; **non-effective, unwarlike,** Eun.Hist.p.239D.; so (with a pun—**not having received an oracle**) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.98c}}. _adverb_ -τως, ἔχειν πρός τι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 61.43.}}
**II** **unkind, cruel,** θεοί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.111; λόγος [prev. author] 9.111.}}
**III** _active_, **making no use of,** with _dative_, ξυνέσει τ᾽ ἄχρηστον τῇ φύσει τε λείπεται{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 672.}}
**IV** **not used,** i.e. **new,** ἱμάτια{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Legal.” 9, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.97e.}}
**IV.2** **obsolete,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 118.25, Scholia recentia 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 132.}}
**IV.3** **not to be used, unseemly,** {{Refs|EM 463.26.}}

891:
** ἄχρι**
and ἄχρις (see. entry near the end):
**I** _adverb_ **to the uttermost,** τένοντε καὶ ὀστέα λᾶας ἀναιδὴς ἄχρις ἀπηλοίησεν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.522; ἀπὸ δ᾽ ὀστέον ἄχρις ἄραξε 16.324, compare 17.599.}}
**I.2** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, before Preps., ἄχρι εἰς Κοτύωρα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.5.4; ἄ. ἐς ποταμόν “Tab.Heracl.” 1.17}}; ἄχρι πρὸς τὸν σκοπόν, πρὸς τὴν πόλιν, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 36, “Herm.” 24; ἄχρις ἐπ᾽ ἄκνηστιν 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1403; ἐπ᾽ ὀστέον “IG” 12(7).115.9 (from Amorgos)}}; ἄχρι ἐπὶ πολὺ τῶν πλευρῶν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 19.3; ἄχρις ἐς ἠῶ 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 6.177; ἄχρι ὑπὸ τὴν πυγήν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 27.4}}: less frequently after the Noun, ἐς τέλος ἄχρις{{Refs 1st c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 2.617, compare 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 5.153, etc.: rarely with _accusative_, ἄχρι.. θρόνον ἦλθεν “IG” 14.2012 (Sulp. 1st c.BC(?): Maximus Astrologus): with an}}_adverb_, ἄχρι πόρρω **still** farther, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 12; ἄχρι δεῦρο 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 8.401.}}
**II** _preposition_ with _genitive_, **even to, as far as,**
**II.1** of Time, **until,** ἄχρι μάλα κνέφαος **until** deep in the night, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.370; ἄχρι τῆς τήμερον ἡμέρας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.28; ἀπὸ τῆς ἀρχῆς ἄχρι τῆς τελευτῆς [prev. author] 18.179; ἄχρι γήρως 4th-3rd c.BC: Apollodorus Comicus 2}}; ἄχρι δὲ τούτου **until** then, {{NT+6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 13.35; ἄχρι τοῦ νῦν 2nd c.BC: Timostratus Comicus 1, NT.Rom.8.22; ἄχρι νῦν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 39, LXX.Gen.44.28; ἄχρι παντός}}**continually,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 6.}}
**II.2** of Space, **as far as, even to,** ἄχρι τῆς ἐσόδου τοῦ ἱροῦ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.138 (who elsewhere has μέχρι); ἄ. τῆς ὁδοῦ “IG” 12.893; ἄ. τῆς πυλίδος “SIG” 2587.25; ἄ. τοῦ Πειραιῶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.301; ἔδακνεν ἄχρι τῆς καρδίας “Comica Adespota” 475; ἄχρις ἥπατος 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 101a, compare 100e; ἄχρι τῆς πόλεως 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.43; ἄ. τοῦ δεῦρο 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.676}}: after its case, ἰνίου ἄχρις{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euphorio Epicus 41.}}
**II.3** of Measure or Degree, ἄχρι τούτου **up to** this point, {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.122; ἄχρι τοῦ μὴ πεινῆν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.37; ἄχρι τοῦ θορυβῆσαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.77; ἄ. θανάτου NT.Act.22.4; ἄχρι τῆς πρὸς τὸν πλησίον δοξοκοπίας Polystr. p.19 W.}}
**III** as _conjunction_, ἄχρι, ἄχρις with or without οὗ,
**III.1** of Time, **until, so long as,** ἄχρι οὗ ὅδε ὁ λόγος ἐγράφετο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.37; ἄχρις ὅτου “epigram Gr.” 314.24 (from Smyrna)}}; ἄχρι οὗ ἄν or ἄχρι ἄν with Subj., ἄχρι ἂν σχολάσῃ **till** he should be at leisure, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.2; ἄχρις οὗ ἂν δοκέῃ 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ συρίγγων” 3; ἄχρις ἂν αἱ ἡμέραι παρέλθωσιν [prev. author] “Int.” 40}}; ἄχρι ἂν ἔχῃ τὸ ἴδιον ἐντελές [ἡ ἱστορία] {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 9: without ἄν, ἄχρις ῥεύσῃ 2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus 1.47}}; ἄχρι οὗ τελευτήσῃ (variant{-σει}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.117; ἄχρι οὗ ἐπιλάμψῃ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 17}}; ἄχρι ἄν, c._infinitive_, Epist.{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Mithradates Epistolographus in “SIG” 741.37}}: with _infinitive_ only, ἄχρις ἱκέσθαι ὀστέον{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 4.361.}}
**III.2** of Space, **so far as,** διώξας, ἄχρι οὗ ἀσφαλὲς ᾤετο εἶναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.16}}: with _subjunctive_, αὐξάνεται εἰς μῆκος, ἄχρι οὗ δὴ ἐφίκηται τοῦ ἡλίου{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 5.1.8}}; compare μέχρι throughout. —_Epic dialect_ poets use ἄχρι or ἄχρις, as the metre requires: in _Ionic dialect_ μέχρι is preferred (see. above): but ἄχρι, -ις are more common in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus than μέχρι: the only _Attic dialect_ forms are ἄχρι, μέχρι}} before both consonants and vowels,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|compare 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 6, 2nd c.AD: Moeris Grammaticus 34}}; and so in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. (where it is somewhat less frequently than μέχρι): ἄχρι ἄν with hiatus in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 2.2729, 3rd c.BC: Hegesippus Comicus 1.26; but ἄχρις 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 179.}} —Never in Trag. (ἄχρι, ={ṃṃ-χρι}, compare μέχρι.)

892:
** ἄχῠρον** [ᾰ], τό,
mostly in _plural_ ἄχυρα, **chaff, bran, husks** left after threshing or grinding, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.72, 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 161, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 226.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 18.1; ἐν τοῖς ἀ. κυλινδομένην 5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 47: _sing._, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.4.1, NT.Matt.3.12, etc.: proverbial, ὄνος εἰς ἄχυρα}} 'pig in clover', of unexpected good fortune, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 188, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 76: _metaphorically_, ἄχυρα τῶν ἀστῶν, of μέτοικοι, [prev. author] “Ach.” 508}}; ἄχυρα ἀπὸ τοῦ τοίχου ἀποσπᾶν, of dying persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 4.}}
**II** in _plural_, ἄ. χρυσοχοϊκά **slag** from gold-smelting, {{Refs|“PHolm.” 5.7.}}

893:
** ἀψευδ-ής**, ές,
**without deceit, truthful,** especially of oracles and the like, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 233, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.49, 2.152 (_superlative_), +others}}; μάντις ἀ., of Apollo, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 559, compare “fragment” 350.5, 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 29 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator (_superlative_)}}; θεός{{NT|NT.Tit.1.2}}; ἀψευδεῖ τέχνῃ, of augury, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 26; ἦθος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 869}}; **unerring,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 160d, etc.; μάρτυς -έστατος 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.341.}}
**2** of things, **uncorrupt,** **pure from all deceit,** ἀ. πρὸς ἄκμονι χάλκευε γλῶσσαν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.86.}}
**3** ἀψευδής, ={κώνειον}, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.78.}}
**II** _adverb_ -δέως, _Attic dialect_-δῶς, **really and truly,** οἱ ἀ. ἄριστοι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.58, compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.19, +others, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 2.2}}: _superlative_ -έστατα, ἐρεῖν{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.34.}}

894:
Included with: ** ἀψίνθ-ιον**, τό,
**wormwood, Artemisia Absinthium,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 3.11, “Mul.” 1.74, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.1, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.12.1, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.23; ἀψινθίῳ κατέπασας Ἀττικὸν μέλι 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 708}}:—also ἄψινθος, ἡ, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “χρονίων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.13, but ὁ, NT.Rev.8.11}}; and ἀψινθία, ἡ, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor Trall. 1.10.}}
**II** ἀψίνθιον, ={ἀβρότονον}, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.24.}}
**II.2** = **Artemisia monosperma,** {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aquila LXX.Pro.5.4.}}
**II.3** ἀ. θαλάσσιον, ={σέριφον}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.23.}}

895:
** ἄψῡχ-ος**, ον,
**lifeless, inanimate,** πόθῳ{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 84; μνημεῖ᾽ ἄψυχ᾽ ἐμψύχων 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 106.4, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 655, “Tr.” 623; λωτὸς ἄ. ἔμπνουν ἀνίει Μοῦσαν 4th c.BC: Sopater Comicus 10}}; ἃ -ότατα τῶν ὀστῶν **with least life** or **sensation,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 74e, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 413a21, etc.}}; ἄ. θεοί, of statues, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Timaeus Historicus 127.}}
**2** ἄ. βορά **non-animal** food, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 952.}}
**II** **spiritless, faint-hearted,** κάκη{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 192; ἀνήρ “Tragica Adespota” 337: ἀψυχότεραι αἱ θήλειαι [ἐλέφαντες] 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 610a21}}; of style, **lifeless,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 20}}. _adverb_ **-χως**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.227.}}
**III** **materialistic,** λόγος{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Gaurum - Abh. Berl. Akad.” 14.4 (_comparative_).}}

898:
** βαθμ-ός**
or **βασμός**, ὁ, (βαίνω) **step, threshold,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.1Ki.5.5, [5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus] “fragment” 1127}}; **degree** on the dial, {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.20.9f}}; **fifteen degrees** of the zodiac, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 31.2}}; **interval** in a musical scale, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 26.120.}}
**2** **rung** of a ladder, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Trag.” 221.}}
**3** **base** or **plinth** of a tower, {{Refs|“GDI” 5524.10 (from Cyzicus).}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **step, degree** in rank (οἱ β. κλίμακος προκοπὴν σημαίνουσι {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 2.42), NT.1Tim.3.13, 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “Historia Arcana” 24, 6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “Mag.” 2.8, +others; οἱ τᾶς ἀξίας βάσμοι “IG” 12(2).243.16 (Mytil.)}}; simply, **degree,** τολμημάτων βαθμοί {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 4.3.10; ὥσπερ ἡδονῆς κλίμακα συμπηξάμενος ἔρως πρῶτον ἔχει β. ὄψεως 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 53}}; **step** in an argument, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis de Caelo commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 718.35}}; of a genealogy, ἀπωτέρω δυοῖν β. two **steps** farther back, i.e. farther back than one's grandfather, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 41.6.}}
**III** **tax paid on stairs,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 574 (2nd c.AD): _accusative_ to 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 296, 2nd c.AD: Moeris Grammaticus 97, βαθμός is _Ionic dialect_, βασμός _Attic dialect_, but βασμός occurs “GDI” 5524.10 (Cyzic.), “Jahresh.” 3.55 (from Scepsis).}}

899:
** βάθος** [ᾰ], εος, τό,
(βαθύς) **depth** or **height,** _accusative_ as measured up or down, Ταρτάρου βάθη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1029; αἰθέρος βάθος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1297, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1715}}; βάθους μετέχειν to be a solid, possessing **depth** as well as length and breadth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 528b; εἴτ᾽ ἐν βάθεσιν εἴτ᾽ ἐν τάχεσιν [prev. author] “Plt.” 299e; βάθους αὔξη [prev. author] “R.” 528d}}; opposed to μῆκος, πλάτος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 209a5; μεγέθους τὸ ἐπὶ τρία [συνεχὲς] β. [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1020a12}}: with Preps., ἐκ βάθεος **in depth,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.186}}; ἐκ βάθους **through and through,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.8.10; εἰς βάθος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 386a23, +others; ἐν βάθει [prev. author] “Sens.” 440a14, etc.; κατὰ βάθους [prev. author] “Mete.” 339b12}}; κατὰ βάθος **in a descending scale,** _metaphorically_ of causation, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 95}}: frequently in military sense, **depth** of a line of battle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.4.13, etc.; ἐπὶ βάθος τάσσεσθαι}}**in depth of line,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.68; ἐς β. ἐκτάξαι 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.4}}; β. τριχῶν, of **long thick** hair, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.9; ἄτομα πώγωνος βάθη 4th c.BC: Ephippus Comicus 14.7}}; **interior** of a country, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.3.7, +others}}; **depth,** of perspective in a picture, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.AD: Procopius Gazaeus Rhetor “ἔκφρασις ὡρολογίου” p.157 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus: _plural_, βάθη}}**depths,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 44d, etc.}}; **deep water,** opposed to shallows near shore, {{LXX+NT+4th c.BC+|LXX.Psa.68(69).2, +others, NT.Luke.5.4; ἐν τοῖς βάθεσιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 599b9.}}
**b** Astron., ={ταπείνωμα}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 241.26.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, κακῶν ὁρῶν β. {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 465; ἢ μακροῦ πλούτου βάθει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 130, cf. NT.Rom.11.33; β. ἡγεμονίας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 53}}; **depth of mind,** β. τι ἔχειν γενναῖον, of Parmenides, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 184a}}; ἐν βάθει πόσιος **deep** in drink, {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.29; β. καρδίας ἀνθρώπου LXX.Jdt.8.14}}; τὰ β. τοῦ θεοῦ, τοῦ Σατανᾶ, {{NT|NT.1Cor.2.10, NT.Rev.2.24.}}
**3** of literal style, **bathos,** ὕψους ἢ β. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 2.1. (Substituted for βένθος under the influence of βαθύς.)}}
